{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01H5WsuouLCPCpVBGRiEwBLq","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Operation Wolfpack\n2\t\n3\t*An original first-person light-gun rail shooter for 1–4 players, inspired by Taito's* Operation Wolf *(1987).*\n4\t*Build plan, written 2026-11-03 for the developers (human or AI) who will build the game from TASK.md and this file alone.*\n5\t\n6\t**How the title was chosen.** TASK.md asks for a playful riff on the source's name, picked with a real random number generator\n7\tfrom 50 titles brainstormed in silence. The planner wrote 50 riffs, ran\n8\t`python3 -c \"import secrets; print(secrets.randbelow(50) + 1)\"`, got **33**, and title #33 was **Operation Wolfpack**. Don't\n9\tpick again. The pun runs through the whole game: the original's lone commando becomes a squad (co-op for 1–4), and the\n10\tenemy is the *Hyena Legion*, so the game is wolves against hyenas.\n11\t\n12\t---\n13\t\n14\t## Contents\n15\t\n16\t1. Deliverables and names\n17\t2. The game in one page\n18\t3. Research summary: what we keep, what we change\n19\t4. Genre, perspective, rendering approach\n20\t5. Controls and prompts\n21\t6. Architecture and modules\n22\t7. Systems (exact rules and numbers)\n23\t8. Content: setting, cast, writing, bestiary, items, the six operations, cutscenes\n24\t9. Presentation: art assets, animation, audio\n25\t10. UI and UX (10-foot), screens, HUD, settings\n26\t11. Dev mode\n27\t12. Attract mode and the bot\n28\t13. Multiplayer: local co-op and AI Hub\n29\t14. Touch-only devices, About/Credits, exit interview\n30\t15. Build order and milestones\n31\t16. Risks and how to de-risk them\n32\t17. Testing\n33\t18. Definition of done\n34\t\n35\t---\n36\t\n37\t## 1. Deliverables and names\n38\t\n39\t| What | Exact name or place |\n40\t|---|---|\n41\t| Title (title screen, `<title>`, hub metas, About, exit interview) | `Operation Wolfpack` |\n42\t| Name tokens | GameTitle = `OperationWolfpack`, SOURCE = `OperationWolf` |\n43\t| Dev folder (every dev file goes here) | `./OperationWolfpack-OperationWolf-claude-opus-5-5-max-20261103-174746/` |\n44\t| The single-file game | `./OperationWolfpack-OperationWolf-claude-opus-5-5-max-20261103-174746.html` (this folder's root, beside TASK.md) |\n45\t| Exit interview | `./exit-interview.json` (root, beside the .html; the exact shape is in TASK.md; check that it parses) |\n46\t| Chat reply when done | Source Information, Testing Feedback, Engine Feedback and Prompt Feedback (TASK.md), the same text as the in-game About/Credits screen |\n47\t\n48\t`./` is the folder holding TASK.md, STAGE.md and PLAN.md. Other agents share the parent `runs/` folder, the machine's ports\n49\tand its processes: don't read their folders, and stop only processes you started, by PID.\n50\t\n51\t**Getting the base.** Engine: the handoff package of my-3D2dge **v0.85.2** (repository commit `4390a98`, 2026-11-03):\n52\t`https://github.com/michaelcrosato/my-3d2dge/tree/main/dist/handoff`. AI Hub client and harness:\n53\t`https://github.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/tree/main/lib/hub` (protocol 1, client **1.4.0**). Fetch both into the dev folder,\n54\tnot `/tmp`: when this plan was written the machine's `/tmp` tmpfs was full. For example, from the dev folder:\n55\t\n56\t```sh\n57\tgit clone --depth 1 --filter=blob:none --sparse https://github.com/michaelcrosato/my-3d2dge.git vendor/my-3d2dge\n58\tgit -C vendor/my-3d2dge sparse-checkout set dist/handoff\n59\tcp -r vendor/my-3d2dge/dist/handoff/. engine/\n60\tmkdir -p hub && cd hub && for f in session.js harness.html bridge.js round.js; do\n61\t  curl -fsSO https://raw.githubusercontent.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/main/lib/hub/$f; done\n62\t```\n63\t\n64\tIf a newer engine version is there, read its `CHANGELOG.md` first. The rest of this plan names the v0.85.2 APIs.\n65\t\n66\t---\n67\t\n68\t## 2. The game in one page\n69\t\n70\t**Pitch.** General Hugo Kragg and his mercenary *Hyena Legion* have seized the Brimstone Coast and taken five relief workers\n71\thostage. No army will go in, so they sent the pack. You are the **Wolfpack**, four commandos with submachine guns, limited\n72\tmagazines and a few grenades. Over six operations, from a radio hill at dawn to a cargo plane escaping a gunship at\n73\tsunrise, you blast through a battlefield that scrolls past in depth, spare the civilians, and bring the hostages home.\n74\t\n75\t**How it plays.** It's a first-person light-gun shooter. The camera rides a scripted rail across each battlefield (sideways,\n76\tlike the source; forward up a river; backward off a plane's ramp). The player aims a free crosshair with the mouse, a\n77\tstick or the keys, and holds the trigger for full-auto fire. Soldiers run, pop out of cover and windows, throw grenades and\n78\tknives, and fire rockets. Vehicles and helicopters come in. Every gun-armed enemy telegraphs its shot with a closing ring:\n79\tkill it before the ring closes, or the damage meter fills. Grenades, knives, rockets and mortar shells can all be shot out\n80\tof the air. Ammo comes in magazines and runs out, so you restock by shooting supply crates and spooking the supply animals\n81\tthe villagers keep. Every operation has an **enemy quota** (soldiers, vehicles, boats, aircraft, shown as counters). Fill\n82\tthe quota, reach the end of the rail, and the operation's boss arrives. Beat it to clear the operation and earn that op's\n83\tperk for the rest of the run.\n84\t\n85\t**A run.** Title → briefing → map → Op 1 Radio Hill → Op 2 Mangrove Run → interrogation → Op 3 Palm Village → Op 4 Powder\n86\tKeg Depot → Op 5 The Kennel (the hostage breakout) → Op 6 Last Flight Out (boarding, takeoff, the gunship) → an ending\n87\tthat depends on how many hostages came home → rank, credits, initials. A first clear takes about 25–30 minutes. Players\n88\t2–4 can drop in at any time with another controller, and in AI Hub sessions 1–4 players fight one operation together\n89\tonline.\n90\t\n91\t**What the player can do:** aim; fire full-auto; reload by hand (or let it reload automatically); throw grenades at the\n92\tcrosshair; on a pad, snap the crosshair to the most dangerous target; shoot pickups to collect them; shoot projectiles out\n93\tof the air; shoot props (crates, red barrels, fuel tanks, masts, searchlights, ropes); protect civilians and hostages by\n94\tkilling what threatens them.\n95\t\n96\t**What the player cannot do:** walk, strafe, duck, take cover or choose a path (the rail is scripted); heal except with\n97\tpickups and stage clears; carry more than the pouch limits; shoot through cover (sandbags, walls, wrecks); pause an online\n98\tsession (the menu opens, the world goes on).\n99\t\n100\t**Pillars.**\n101\t1. **Every bullet counts:** magazines, reload timing, supply crates, the DRY rule.\n102\t2. **Read the battlefield in depth:** near knife-throwers, far snipers, closing rings, shootable projectiles.\n103\t3. **Don't shoot the innocent:** civilians and hostages cross the line of fire; hitting them costs damage and points.\n104\t4. **Pack tactics:** 1–4 seat-colored crosshairs, shared quotas, rings colored by who is targeted.\n105\t5. **80s arcade punch:** terse all-caps cards, big explosions, a score chase, initials, an attract mode.\n106\t\n107\t---\n108\t\n109\t## 3. Research summary: what we keep, what we change\n110\t\n111\t**Facts about the source** (Wikipedia, arcade-history.com): an arcade game by Taito, released in 1987, played with a\n112\tmounted Uzi-style gun that vibrates as it fires. A special forces operative rescues five hostages across six stages:\n113\tCommunication Setup, Jungle, Village, Powder Magazine, Concentration Camp and Airport. The trigger fires full-auto, and a\n114\tbutton launches a grenade with a wide blast. Each stage requires a set number of soldiers and vehicles (trucks, boats,\n115\thelicopters, armored transports), shown on counters. Ammo and grenades are limited; you replenish them by shooting objects\n116\tand passing farm animals. A machine-gun power-up gives unlimited ammo and faster fire for 10 s. Dynamite damages\n117\teverything on screen, friend and foe. A damage bar fills when you are hit or shoot civilians or hostages. Clearing the\n118\tVillage restores a lot of damage, clearing the Powder Magazine refills ammo and grenades, and the other stages restore a\n119\tlittle. After the jungle the hero interrogates a soldier, who gives up the camp's location. In the camp you protect\n120\thostages as they run, and at the airport they run for the plane, with a bonus per hostage aboard. Big machine gunners die\n121\tfast to shots in the face, and mortar crews sit behind sandbags. Enemy projectiles can be shot down. The game ends when the\n122\tdamage bar fills or when both ammo and grenades run out. After the ending it loops at a higher difficulty.\n123\t\n124\t| We keep (what makes the source itself) | We change (to make it original, modern and fair) |\n125\t|---|---|\n126\t| A crosshair over a battlefield scrolling past in depth | An original setting, cast and stage names (Brimstone Coast, Hyena Legion, Wolfpack) |\n127\t| A full-auto SMG with limited magazines; grenades with a big blast | A boss at the end of every operation, with weak points and phases |\n128\t| A damage meter that fills; damage repaired on stage clears | A closing **telegraph ring** before every enemy shot, so damage is readable and avoidable |\n129\t| Enemy quota counters per stage | Co-op for 1–4, locally and in AI Hub sessions |\n130\t| Supplies from crates, barrels and animals | Animals are never killed: a hit makes them drop what they carry and flee |\n131\t| A 10 s machine-gun power-up (**FURY**); **DYNAMITE** that hurts everyone on screen | No child civilians; a green cross, not the Red Cross emblem; no blood (dust, flashes, falls) |\n132\t| Civilian and hostage penalties; hostage escorts in the camp and at the airfield | A streak multiplier, aim assist for pads, three difficulty levels |\n133\t| Six stages: comms → jungle → village (heal) → ammo dump (refill) → camp → airport | Out of ammo and grenades ends the run only on ARCADE difficulty; on the others an emergency drop comes |\n134\t| The interrogation beat; endings that depend on hostages saved; loop 2 after the ending | First-person SMG overlay, shell casings, rumble, a gibberish-voiced radio |\n135\t\n136\t---\n137\t\n138\t## 4. Genre, perspective, rendering approach\n139\t\n140\t- **Genre:** light-gun rail shooter, the shooting-gallery kind (catalogue genre: *Shooter*).\n141\t- **Perspective:** first person. The camera sits on a scripted rail with a fixed heading per segment, at eye height. The\n142\t  crosshair is free; the camera never follows it.\n143\t- **Rendering:** the engine's **Mode 7 module** (`E.Mode7`, part of the full engine `my-3d2dge.js`): a perspective floor\n144\t  drawn a row at a time, a `E.Backdrop` sky, and billboards drawn far to near at the size their distance gives. That means\n145\t  HD Humanoid rigs (`m7.rig`), shapes-as-data (`m7.shape`: vehicles, buildings and props seen from the right side and\n146\t  facing), string sprites (`m7.sprite`), and our own drawing (`m7.add(x, y, z, (g, sx, sy, k) => …)`, where `k` is screen\n147\t  pixels per world unit there). This is the API card's light-gun recipe on the engine's own depth view (the 3D engine is not\n148\t  in the handoff), and it gives real parallax: the camera moves sideways, and near things pass faster than far ones.\n149\t- **Resolution:** `res: [480, 270]`, a fixed 16:9 buffer with **no `expand`**. Every player, screen and test then shares\n150\t  one aim space; a 1080p TV is exactly ×4, and 4K is ×8. Leave `game.settings.scale` on `'auto'` (sharp on desktops;\n151\t  players can pick fill). The page's bars use a CSS background: dark olive with a faint repeating wolf emblem, like\n152\t  cabinet side art.\n153\t- **Coordinates:** world units, 16 = 1 m; x east, y south, z up. Most rails run along +x at y = 0 with the camera looking\n154\t  north (`angle = -Math.PI / 2`); depth ahead means negative y. Camera eye `h` is 22–28, `pitch` 4–8°, and `fov` 60°\n155\t  (vertical). At 480×270 with fov 60, the focal length is `f = 135 / tan(30°) ≈ 234 px`. A rig 34 units tall stands about\n156\t  130 px high at depth 60 (a foreground threat), 20 px at 400, and 9 px at 900. The visible half-width at depth D is about\n157\t  1.03 × D.\n158\t\n159\t---\n160\t\n161\t## 5. Controls and prompts\n162\t\n163\tThe action map is a custom preset, `WP.INPUT`, passed to `new E.Game({ input })`; the menu actions come from the engine's\n164\town menu keys. Every action except the menu ones can be rebound on the standard Controls screen.\n165\t\n166\t| Action | DualSense (default glyphs) | Xbox glyphs (setting) | Keyboard + mouse | Keyboard only |\n167\t|---|---|---|---|---|\n168\t| Aim | Left stick (fast) + right stick (half speed, fine), d-pad nudges | same | Mouse (absolute, like a light gun) | WASD / arrow keys |\n169\t| Fire (hold = full-auto) | R2 (`Pad7`) | RT | Left click (`Mouse0`) | Space or J |\n170\t| Grenade (thrown at the crosshair) | L2 (`Pad6`) or R1 (`Pad5`) | LT / RB | Right click (`Mouse2`) or G | K or G |\n171\t| Reload | □ (`Pad2`) | X | R or middle click (`Mouse1`) | R or L |\n172\t| Snap to threat (aim assist) | L1 (`Pad4`) | LB | Q | Q |\n173\t| Pause / menu | Options (`Pad9`) | Menu | Esc or P | Esc or P |\n174\t| Menu confirm / back | ✕ / ○ | A / B | Click / Esc, Enter / Backspace | Enter / Esc |\n175\t| Join (local co-op) | Options on a controller nobody holds | Menu | n/a | n/a |\n176\t\n177\t```js\n178\tWP.INPUT = {\n179\t  up: ['KeyW', 'ArrowUp', 'Pad12'], down: ['KeyS', 'ArrowDown', 'Pad13'], left: ['KeyA', 'ArrowLeft', 'Pad14'], right: ['KeyD', 'ArrowRight', 'Pad15'],\n180\t  fire: ['Mouse0', 'Space', 'KeyJ', 'Pad7'], grenade: ['Mouse2', 'KeyG', 'KeyK', 'Pad6', 'Pad5'],\n181\t  reload: ['KeyR', 'KeyL', 'Mouse1', 'Pad2'], snap: ['KeyQ', 'Pad4'],\n182\t  start: ['Enter', 'NumpadEnter', 'Pad9'], pause: ['Escape', 'KeyP', 'Pad9'],\n183\t  confirm: ['Enter', 'NumpadEnter', 'Space', 'KeyJ', 'Pad0'], cancel: ['Escape', 'Backspace', 'Pad1']\n184\t};\n185\t```\n186\t\n187\t- **The crosshair from keys:** read the movement keys from `input.held` (`KeyW`, `KeyA`, `KeyS`, `KeyD`, `Arrow*`), not\n188\t  from the `up`/`down`/`left`/`right` actions. The engine presses those actions when a stick passes halfway, which would\n189\t  count the stick twice.\n190\t- **Mouse:** the crosshair is `input.pointer()`, in the buffer's own pixels. Hide the OS cursor over the canvas in play\n191\t  (`canvas.style.cursor = 'none'`) and show it in menus. In menus the mouse hovers to move the focus and clicks to pick\n192\t  (`E.Menu` and `E.Shell` do both). On the map, hovering an operation node focuses it.\n193\t- **Prompts** always come from `input.label(action)`, which follows `input.lastDevice`: \"R2\" / \"RT\" on a pad, \"Left\n194\t  click\" after mouse use, \"Space\" after keyboard use. HUD prompts are short, glyph first: `□ RELOAD`, `L2 GRENADE`.\n195\t- **Glyphs:** PlayStation by default, with an Xbox option. Change the engine's standard `glyphs` setting (see 6.5) so its\n196\t  default is `'playstation'` and its options are PlayStation and Xbox only, and add a mirror in Settings → Game\n197\t  (\"BUTTON SYMBOLS\") that calls `settings.set('glyphs', v)`. The engine's own entry lives on the Controls screen; the task\n198\t  asks for it in Settings.\n199\t- **Rumble** (`input.rumble`, per player's pad): each shot .06 strength for .03 s; your grenade .45 for .2 s; taking\n200\t  damage .8 for .25 s; a boss explosion 1 for .6 s.\n201\t- **Hub sessions:** ignore Options while Create (`Pad8`) is held. Create + Options belongs to the hub.\n202\t\n203\t---\n204\t\n205\t## 6. Architecture and modules\n206\t\n207\t### 6.1 Folder layout (inside the dev folder)\n208\t\n209\t```\n210\tpackage.json         scripts: test, build, check; devDependency playwright 1.63.0 (the handoff's pin)\n211\tmanifest.json        the script order (engine first, then src/*) used by dev/index.html, the tests and the bundler\n212\tengine/              the handoff copy: my-3d2dge.js (the full engine, edited), modules/, tools/, docs/, API.md, API-INDEX.md, AI_GUIDE.md\n213\tengine/CHANGES.md    every engine edit and why (it feeds the Engine Feedback answers)\n214\thub/                 session.js (client 1.4.0, never edited), harness.html, bridge.js, round.js (harness files: tests only)\n215\tsrc/                 the game: plain scripts sharing one namespace, WP (list below)\n216\tdev/index.html       loads hub/session.js, engine/my-3d2dge.js and src/* one by one (works over file://)\n217\ttools/build.mjs      bundles everything into ../OperationWolfpack-OperationWolf-claude-opus-5-5-max-20261103-174746.html\n218\ttests/               run.mjs (the one command) and the suites (section 17)\n219\tshots/               screenshots and filmstrips for visual review only (never bundled)\n220\tvendor/              the clone the engine was copied from (not bundled)\n221\t```\n222\t\n223\t### 6.2 Source files (`src/`, loaded in this order)\n224\t\n225\tEvery file starts `(() => { const E = My3D2dge, WP = globalThis.WP; ... })();` and adds to `WP`. No ES modules: the\n226\theadless tests load the files into a `node:vm` context, as the handoff's `game/test.mjs` does.\n227\t\n228\t| File | Owns |\n229\t|---|---|\n230\t| `00-core.js` | `globalThis.WP = {}`; constants (`W = 480`, `H = 270`, `PLAY_H = 234`); seat colors; small utils; the event bus |\n231\t| `01-tuning.js` | `WP.T` (live tuning values) and `WP.TUNING_SPEC` (defaults, ranges, labels: 11.1); difficulty presets; co-op scaling |\n232\t| `02-text.js` | `WP.TEXT`: every visible string (cards, radio, cutscenes, endings, HUD words, help, About). Nothing hard-coded elsewhere |\n233\t| `03-rng.js` | seeded streams from `E.rng`: `sim`, `loot`, `ai`, `fx`; hub seeding |\n234\t| `10-view3d.js` | `WP.View3D`: Mode 7 math for the sim (`begin`, `project`, `toFloor`, `halfWidth(depth)`), camera basis, camera-anchored → world |\n235\t| `11-rail.js` | `WP.Rail`: keyframes → `E.Path` (catmull), speed, holds, tweens of yaw/pitch/h/fov, bob, segment cuts |\n236\t| `12-hit.js` | hit shapes per entity in screen space, tests nearest first, cover occlusion, explosion queries |\n237\t| `20-entities.js` | the entity store (ids, kinds), the `WP.TYPES` registry, shared update (anchors, timers, death, fade, despawn) |\n238\t| `21-infantry.js` | infantry behaviors (7.6) |\n239\t| `22-enemyfire.js` | telegraphs, target choice, hitscan accuracy, damage to players |\n240\t| `23-projectiles.js` | grenades, knives, rockets, missiles, mortar and tank shells, flame waves: motion, shoot-down, impact |\n241\t| `24-vehicles.js` | jeep, truck, APC, tank, skiff, gunboat, helicopter, cargo plane (scenery), weak points, wrecks |\n242\t| `25-bosses.js` | the six bosses (8.7): phases, weak points, attacks, intros, defeats |\n243\t| `26-friendlies.js` | civilians, hostages (escort, capture, recapture), supply animals, friendly markers |\n244\t| `27-props.js` | destructibles and cover: crates, red barrels (chains), sandbags, masts, fuel tanks, searchlights, ropes, ammo wagons, window and door openings |\n245\t| `28-pickups.js` | pickup kinds, drops, bounce and expiry, collection, the supply director, the emergency drop |\n246\t| `30-players.js` | player state, device adapters → `InputFrame`, crosshair motion, aim assist, snap, join, down, return at the next op |\n247\t| `31-weapon.js` | SMG (rate, bloom, magazines, reload, FURY), grenade throws, dynamite, shot resolution → damage, score, events |\n248\t| `32-scoring.js` | scores, streaks, accuracy, the stage tally, ranks, high scores (through `E.Arcade`) |\n249\t| `33-sim.js` | `WP.Sim`: one operation's run. `step(dt, frames)`, beats and spawns, quotas, reinforcements, boss trigger, clear or fail, `events`, `snapshot()` |\n250\t| `34-mirror.js` | `WP.Mirror`: a hub client's world, rebuilt from snapshots and events, with the same read interface as `WP.Sim` |\n251\t| `40-stages-common.js` | layout helpers (rows of palms, fence runs, sandbag lines, hut rows, tree walls), floor painters, sky specs, beat macros |\n252\t| `41-op1.js` … `46-op6.js` | the stage data, one operation per file (8.6) |\n253\t| `47-cutscenes.js` | briefing, interrogation, Kennel aftermath, takeoff cut, ending variants, as scripts |\n254\t| `50-art-shapes.js` | new `E.SHAPES` entries, materials and patterns (9.1) |\n255\t| `51-art-rigs.js` | Humanoid and Blob presets per type and cast member, the rig pool, custom poses (9.2) |\n256\t| `52-art-sprites.js` | string sprites (pickups, projectiles, icons, emblems) and procedural sprites (muzzle flashes, explosion frames) |\n257\t| `53-fx.js` | world FX particles (drawn through `m7.add`), floor decals; screen FX (grade, darkness mask, rain, vignette, flashes, hit markers, floating numbers, threat arrows) |\n258\t| `54-render.js` | the frame: gather scenery (culled), entities and FX → `m7.draw`; post; telegraph rings; crosshairs |\n259\t| `55-gun.js` | the first-person SMG overlay: model, recoil, reload, FURY glow, grenade hand |\n260\t| `60-audio.js` | sound definitions, loops (rotors, engines, rain), the mixer, the rumble map |\n261\t| `61-music.js` | songs as data; the music director (layers by intensity, ducking under radio lines) |\n262\t| `62-voice.js` | the gibberish voice synth, speaker profiles, barks, the howl, radio crackle; the Dialog hook |\n263\t| `70-hud.js` | the canvas HUD (10.3) |\n264\t| `71-screens.js` | canvas screens: title, squad, map, briefing, op card, tally, continue, ending, credits, initials, About, the touch notice, the hub card |\n265\t| `72-shell.js` | the `E.Shell` theme, 10-foot CSS, pause items, game settings, help |\n266\t| `73-devmode.js` | the dev screen and the dev overlay (section 11) |\n267\t| `80-bot.js` | `WP.Bot`, the AI player (attract mode, autopilot, tests) |\n268\t| `81-attract.js` | the attract playlist and controller (`E.Arcade` phases) |\n269\t| `90-net.js` | the AI Hub session: roles, card, start, inputs, snapshots, events, interpolation, finish (13.2) |\n270\t| `91-codec.js` | compact encoding, the size guard, the rate limiter |\n271\t| `95-scenes.js` | the scene table, flow and deep links (6.4) |\n272\t| `99-main.js` | `new E.Game`, boot steps, settings, the debug handle, start |\n273\t\n274\t### 6.3 Layers and the one rule that keeps them testable\n275\t\n276\t- **The Sim** (`WP.Sim` and `20`–`33`) holds all game state and rules. It is deterministic: fixed steps, its own seeded\n277\t  streams, no `Math.random`, DOM, audio or rigs. Each step it takes one `InputFrame` per player:\n278\t  `{ x, y (crosshair in buffer px, floats), fire (held), grenade, reload, snap (presses this step), device }`. Every\n279\t  noteworthy thing becomes an event in `sim.events` (`{ k: 'kill', id, by, part }`, `shot`, `hit`, `pickup`, `damage`,\n280\t  `explode`, `spawn`, `telegraph`, `radio`, `boss`, `clear`, …).\n281\t- **Presentation** (`50`–`72`) reads a world (a `WP.Sim`, or a `WP.Mirror` on a hub client) and consumes its events for\n282\t  sound, FX, rig poses and the HUD. Rig posing happens in `update` (the engine's rule: draw only draws), but it is\n283\t  cosmetic and never feeds back into the Sim.\n284\t- **Players** (`30`) turn devices, the bot or the network into `InputFrame`s. Tests can inject frames directly:\n285\t  `WP.Players.override(pid, fn)`.\n286\t- **Net** (`90`, `91`) moves `InputFrame`s from clients to the host, and snapshots and events from the host to clients.\n287\t\n288\tHit tests run inside the Sim, on the Sim's own `WP.View3D` (a second `E.Mode7` used only for its math:\n289\t`m7.begin({ W: 480, H: 270 }, cam)`, then `project` and `toFloor`). It is built from the same camera as the renderer's\n290\t`E.Mode7`, so what you see is what you hit, headless included.\n291\t\n292\t### 6.4 Scenes, flow, deep links\n293\t\n294\tScenes (`game.start({ scenes, scene: 'boot', boot: { steps } })`):\n295\t`boot` → `hubcard` (sessions only) or `title` ⇄ `attract` (demo, then the high-score table) → `squad` (optional co-op lobby) →\n296\t`briefing` → `map` → `opcard` → `play` (`pausable: true`, except in sessions) → `tally` → (`cutscene`) → `map` → … →\n297\t`ending` → `credits` → `initials` → `title`. Also `continue` (game over), `about`, `range` (dev target practice), and\n298\t`touch` (the touch-only notice, laid over `title`).\n299\t\n300\tDeep links, for the tools and tests (read from `location.hash` at boot): `#title`, `#attract`, `#about`, `#map`, `#op1` …\n301\t`#op6`, `#op3/boss`, `#op6/b` (the takeoff segment), `#cut/interrogation`, `#ending/0` … `#ending/5`, `#range`, `#dev`\n302\t(play with the dev screen open).\n303\t\n304\t### 6.5 Engine usage and engine changes\n305\t\n306\tWhat the game uses from the engine: `E.Game` (fixed 1/120 s steps, scenes, fades, `flash`/`shake`/`freeze`/`timeScale`,\n307\t`script`/`tween`/`after`/`every`, `advance` and `debug` for tests), `E.Mode7`, `E.Backdrop`, `E.Humanoid` and `E.MOVES`,\n308\t`E.Blob`, `E.SHAPES` with `E.MATERIALS` and `E.PATTERNS` (`docs/ASSETS.md`), `E.sprite`/`E.anim`/`E.recolor`, `E.px.*`\n309\tincluding `px.blend`, `px.ddisc` and `px.polyDither`, `E.font`, `E.ui`, `E.Dialog` (portraits, non-modal radio), `E.Menu`,\n310\t`E.Shell` (pause, settings, controls, help, the dev screen), `game.settings` (`define`, `set`, `reset`), `game.input`\n311\t(`pressed`, `down`, `held`, `pointer`, `padMove`, `padAim`, `label`, `glyphs`, `lastDevice`, `rumble`, and `tap`/`hold`/\n312\t`feed` for tests), `game.allowJoin`/`addPlayer`/`players`, `E.Arcade` (cards, tables, initials, continue, attract phases),\n313\t`game.audio` (`sfx`, `define`, `music` with tracks and layers, `duck`, `sting`), `E.rng`, `E.noise2`, `E.tex.*`, `E.arc`,\n314\t`E.Path`, `E.store`, `E.versionLabel`. Look names up in `API-INDEX.md` (grep it; don't read it whole); `API.md` is the\n315\tcard to read whole.\n316\t\n317\tEngine edits, each logged in `engine/CHANGES.md`:\n318\t\n319\t1. **Dialog voice as a function.** At the per-letter blip in `Dialog.update` (around line 8956 of v0.85.2:\n320\t   `if (this.voice && …) this.game.audio.sfx(this.voice)`), when `typeof this.voice === 'function'`, call\n321\t   `this.voice(ch, index, this)` instead. That is the hook for gibberish voices.\n322\t2. **Shell rows screen.** `shell.show(name, spec)` gets `spec.rows`, a list of `[heading, [settingKeys]]` drawn with the\n323\t   existing `_row(key)` (sliders, toggles, selects, with pad and keyboard focus), plus `spec.actions` buttons. The\n324\t   standard Settings screen skips setting groups listed in `E.Shell.hiddenGroups` (set it to `['Dev']`).\n325\t3. **A non-pausing menu for sessions.** `new E.Game({ menu: { pauses: false } })`: the pause and settings screens open\n326\t   over a running game, which keeps calling `update`. The game's input is held back while a screen is open, as it is now.\n327\t4. **Glyph setting.** The standard `glyphs` setting (around line 2999) defaults to `'playstation'`, with options\n328\t   `[['playstation', 'PlayStation'], ['xbox', 'Xbox']]`.\n329\t5. **Stereo pan (optional, nice to have).** `audio.sfx(voice, { pan: -1..1 })` through a `StereoPannerNode`, to place\n330\t   gunfire, rotors and explosions left and right.\n331\t6. **Anything else you find missing:** extend it in the engine and log it. Don't work around a bug in game code without\n332\t   logging it either.\n333\t\n334\t`My3D2dge` loads headless in Node (no DOM). The game must too: guard every DOM use (`typeof document !== 'undefined'`),\n335\tand keep the Sim free of it.\n336\t\n337\t### 6.6 Data formats\n338\t\n339\t**Stage definition** (one per `src/4x-opN.js`, pushed onto `WP.STAGES`):\n340\t\n341\t```js\n342\tWP.STAGES.push({\n343\t  id: 'op1', n: 1, name: 'RADIO HILL', card: 'DESTROY THE ENEMY RADIO STATION', music: 'op1', time: 'dawn',\n344\t  sky: { sky: ['#1d1a3a', '#7a3f6e', '#e88a6a', '#f6c48c'], sun: { x: .7, y: .62, r: 18, color: '#ffd6a0' },\n345\t         layers: [{ kind: 'mountains', color: '#5a3a5e', y: .58, height: .12, parallax: .1 }, { kind: 'sea', color: '#8a5a7a', y: .66 }] },\n346\t  fog: { color: '#e0a07c', near: 320, far: 1600 }, grade: { mul: '#ffe6d6', add: '#100008' },\n347\t  floor: 'hill',                        // a painter in WP.FLOORS: (x, y, t) => [r, g, b]\n348\t  cam: { h: 28, pitch: 6, fov: 60 },\n349\t  rail: [{ d: 0, x: 0, y: 0, angle: -Math.PI / 2 }, { d: 2700, x: 2700, y: 0 }],   // knots: position and tweened angle/h/pitch/fov\n350\t  speed: 16,                            // units a second, times WP.T.railSpeed\n351\t  holds: [{ d: 520, until: 'beat:b6', max: 15 }, { d: 1720, until: 'air', max: 30 }, { d: 2700, until: 'boss' }],\n352\t  scenery: [['shape', 'radioMast', 900, -380, 0, { facing: 0 }], ['row', 'palm', 0, 2700, -900, { every: 60, jitter: 20 }]],\n353\t  props: [['sandbags', 'sbA', 160, -110], ['crate', 'c1', 420, -120, { drop: 'mag' }], ['mast', 'm1', 900, -380]],\n354\t  beats: [{ id: 'b1', at: 0, radio: 'op1.go' },\n355\t          { id: 'b2', at: 40, spawn: [['rifleman', { side: 'R', dz: 200, n: 3, gap: .8, path: 'cross' }]] }],   // ids follow 8.6's table order\n356\t  quotas: { soldier: 36, vehicle: 3, air: 1 }, objective: { kind: 'masts', ids: ['m1', 'm2', 'm3'] },\n357\t  boss: 'bigEar', perk: 'commsDown', clearHeal: 10, animals: ['goat'],\n358\t  attract: [{ from: 'b4', seconds: 25 }]  // the attract mode's segment of this op\n359\t});\n360\t```\n361\t\n362\t- Spawn positions are given **in the camera's frame at the moment the beat fires**: `lx` (sideways, + right), `dz` (depth\n363\t  ahead), `z`. `side: 'L' | 'R'` puts the spawn just off that edge: `lx = ∓(halfWidth(dz) + 30)`. They become world\n364\t  positions at once, so they parallax, unless `anchor: 'cam'`, which keeps them in the camera's frame (river pursuers, the\n365\t  takeoff chase, the gunship).\n366\t- Paths: a named behavior (`'cross'`, `'popup'`, …) or a list of `[lx, dz]` waypoints in the same frame. Cover references\n367\t  name a prop id.\n368\t- Beats fire when the rail passes `at`, or `after: 'b5'` (that beat is cleared), plus an optional `delay`.\n369\t\n370\t**Type registry** (`WP.TYPES[id]`): `{ kind: 'infantry' | 'vehicle' | 'air' | 'boat' | 'boss' | 'civilian' | 'hostage' |\n371\t'animal' | 'projectile' | 'pickup' | 'prop', quota: 'soldier' | 'vehicle' | 'air' | 'boat' | null, hp, score, h (height\n372\tin units), rig | shape | sprite, behavior, fire: { telegraph, bullets, accuracy, damage } | throws: {...}, weak: [...] }`.\n373\tThe bestiary tables in section 8 are the source of these values.\n374\t\n375\t**Text:** `WP.TEXT['op1.go'] = { who: 'DEN', text: 'OPERATION WOLFPACK IS GO. GOOD HUNTING.' }`. Speakers map to voice\n376\tprofiles and portraits.\n377\t\n378\t**Designer tools.** Stage data are plain arrays, and the dev screen can print a ready-to-paste spawn or prop line for the\n379\tpoint under the crosshair (section 11). New operations are new data files; new enemies are new `WP.TYPES` entries with a\n380\tbehavior. That is what lets the rest of the game be built out later with normal designer tools.\n381\t\n382\t### 6.7 The debug handle (for the tests and the engine's tools)\n383\t\n384\t```js\n385\tgame.debug = {\n386\t  snapshot: () => ({ scene, op, beat, d, hold, quotas, boss: { id, phase, hp }, players: [{ pid, x, y, damage, mag, spares, grenades, score, streak, down }],\n387\t                     counts: { enemies, projectiles, pickups, civilians, hostages }, hostagesSaved, events: lastN, dev: changedKeys, errors: game.errors.length }),\n388\t  jump: (op, beatOrD) => {}, cheat: (name, on) => {}, tune: (key, value) => {}, spawn: (type, opts) => id,\n389\t  aim: (pid, x, y) => {}, frames: (pid, fn) => {}, sim: () => currentSim, hub: () => WP.Net.state\n390\t};\n391\t```\n392\t\n393\t---\n394\t\n395\t## 7. Systems (exact rules and numbers)\n396\t\n397\tNumbers are VETERAN defaults. The live ones live in `WP.T`; section 11 lists the sliders. The difficulty and co-op\n398\tmultipliers of 7.16 apply on top.\n399\t\n400\t### 7.1 Rail camera (`11-rail.js`)"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"a0704575-f16b-44ea-a24d-907e9c278025","uuid":"241aeacc-93f7-414f-8119-325dfeb04961","timestamp":"2026-11-04T01:30:19.847Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-xhigh.build__20261103-183009__xe162c9/work/PLAN.md","content":"# Operation Wolfpack\n\n*An original first-person light-gun rail shooter for 1–4 players, inspired by Taito's* Operation Wolf *(1987).*\n*Build plan, written 2026-11-03 for the developers (human or AI) who will build the game from TASK.md and this file alone.*\n\n**How the title was chosen.** TASK.md asks for a playful riff on the source's name, picked with a real random number generator\nfrom 50 titles brainstormed in silence. The planner wrote 50 riffs, ran\n`python3 -c \"import secrets; print(secrets.randbelow(50) + 1)\"`, got **33**, and title #33 was **Operation Wolfpack**. Don't\npick again. The pun runs through the whole game: the original's lone commando becomes a squad (co-op for 1–4), and the\nenemy is the *Hyena Legion*, so the game is wolves against hyenas.\n\n---\n\n## Contents\n\n1. Deliverables and names\n2. The game in one page\n3. Research summary: what we keep, what we change\n4. Genre, perspective, rendering approach\n5. Controls and prompts\n6. Architecture and modules\n7. Systems (exact rules and numbers)\n8. Content: setting, cast, writing, bestiary, items, the six operations, cutscenes\n9. Presentation: art assets, animation, audio\n10. UI and UX (10-foot), screens, HUD, settings\n11. Dev mode\n12. Attract mode and the bot\n13. Multiplayer: local co-op and AI Hub\n14. Touch-only devices, About/Credits, exit interview\n15. Build order and milestones\n16. Risks and how to de-risk them\n17. Testing\n18. Definition of done\n\n---\n\n## 1. Deliverables and names\n\n| What | Exact name or place |\n|---|---|\n| Title (title screen, `<title>`, hub metas, About, exit interview) | `Operation Wolfpack` |\n| Name tokens | GameTitle = `OperationWolfpack`, SOURCE = `OperationWolf` |\n| Dev folder (every dev file goes here) | `./OperationWolfpack-OperationWolf-claude-opus-5-5-max-20261103-174746/` |\n| The single-file game | `./OperationWolfpack-OperationWolf-claude-opus-5-5-max-20261103-174746.html` (this folder's root, beside TASK.md) |\n| Exit interview | `./exit-interview.json` (root, beside the .html; the exact shape is in TASK.md; check that it parses) |\n| Chat reply when done | Source Information, Testing Feedback, Engine Feedback and Prompt Feedback (TASK.md), the same text as the in-game About/Credits screen |\n\n`./` is the folder holding TASK.md, STAGE.md and PLAN.md. Other agents share the parent `runs/` folder, the machine's ports\nand its processes: don't read their folders, and stop only processes you started, by PID.\n\n**Getting the base.** Engine: the handoff package of my-3D2dge **v0.85.2** (repository commit `4390a98`, 2026-11-03):\n`https://github.com/michaelcrosato/my-3d2dge/tree/main/dist/handoff`. AI Hub client and harness:\n`https://github.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/tree/main/lib/hub` (protocol 1, client **1.4.0**). Fetch both into the dev folder,\nnot `/tmp`: when this plan was written the machine's `/tmp` tmpfs was full. For example, from the dev folder:\n\n```sh\ngit clone --depth 1 --filter=blob:none --sparse https://github.com/michaelcrosato/my-3d2dge.git vendor/my-3d2dge\ngit -C vendor/my-3d2dge sparse-checkout set dist/handoff\ncp -r vendor/my-3d2dge/dist/handoff/. engine/\nmkdir -p hub && cd hub && for f in session.js harness.html bridge.js round.js; do\n  curl -fsSO https://raw.githubusercontent.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/main/lib/hub/$f; done\n```\n\nIf a newer engine version is there, read its `CHANGELOG.md` first. The rest of this plan names the v0.85.2 APIs.\n\n---\n\n## 2. The game in one page\n\n**Pitch.** General Hugo Kragg and his mercenary *Hyena Legion* have seized the Brimstone Coast and taken five relief workers\nhostage. No army will go in, so they sent the pack. You are the **Wolfpack**, four commandos with submachine guns, limited\nmagazines and a few grenades. Over six operations, from a radio hill at dawn to a cargo plane escaping a gunship at\nsunrise, you blast through a battlefield that scrolls past in depth, spare the civilians, and bring the hostages home.\n\n**How it plays.** It's a first-person light-gun shooter. The camera rides a scripted rail across each battlefield (sideways,\nlike the source; forward up a river; backward off a plane's ramp). The player aims a free crosshair with the mouse, a\nstick or the keys, and holds the trigger for full-auto fire. Soldiers run, pop out of cover and windows, throw grenades and\nknives, and fire rockets. Vehicles and helicopters come in. Every gun-armed enemy telegraphs its shot with a closing ring:\nkill it before the ring closes, or the damage meter fills. Grenades, knives, rockets and mortar shells can all be shot out\nof the air. Ammo comes in magazines and runs out, so you restock by shooting supply crates and spooking the supply animals\nthe villagers keep. Every operation has an **enemy quota** (soldiers, vehicles, boats, aircraft, shown as counters). Fill\nthe quota, reach the end of the rail, and the operation's boss arrives. Beat it to clear the operation and earn that op's\nperk for the rest of the run.\n\n**A run.** Title → briefing → map → Op 1 Radio Hill → Op 2 Mangrove Run → interrogation → Op 3 Palm Village → Op 4 Powder\nKeg Depot → Op 5 The Kennel (the hostage breakout) → Op 6 Last Flight Out (boarding, takeoff, the gunship) → an ending\nthat depends on how many hostages came home → rank, credits, initials. A first clear takes about 25–30 minutes. Players\n2–4 can drop in at any time with another controller, and in AI Hub sessions 1–4 players fight one operation together\nonline.\n\n**What the player can do:** aim; fire full-auto; reload by hand (or let it reload automatically); throw grenades at the\ncrosshair; on a pad, snap the crosshair to the most dangerous target; shoot pickups to collect them; shoot projectiles out\nof the air; shoot props (crates, red barrels, fuel tanks, masts, searchlights, ropes); protect civilians and hostages by\nkilling what threatens them.\n\n**What the player cannot do:** walk, strafe, duck, take cover or choose a path (the rail is scripted); heal except with\npickups and stage clears; carry more than the pouch limits; shoot through cover (sandbags, walls, wrecks); pause an online\nsession (the menu opens, the world goes on).\n\n**Pillars.**\n1. **Every bullet counts:** magazines, reload timing, supply crates, the DRY rule.\n2. **Read the battlefield in depth:** near knife-throwers, far snipers, closing rings, shootable projectiles.\n3. **Don't shoot the innocent:** civilians and hostages cross the line of fire; hitting them costs damage and points.\n4. **Pack tactics:** 1–4 seat-colored crosshairs, shared quotas, rings colored by who is targeted.\n5. **80s arcade punch:** terse all-caps cards, big explosions, a score chase, initials, an attract mode.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Research summary: what we keep, what we change\n\n**Facts about the source** (Wikipedia, arcade-history.com): an arcade game by Taito, released in 1987, played with a\nmounted Uzi-style gun that vibrates as it fires. A special forces operative rescues five hostages across six stages:\nCommunication Setup, Jungle, Village, Powder Magazine, Concentration Camp and Airport. The trigger fires full-auto, and a\nbutton launches a grenade with a wide blast. Each stage requires a set number of soldiers and vehicles (trucks, boats,\nhelicopters, armored transports), shown on counters. Ammo and grenades are limited; you replenish them by shooting objects\nand passing farm animals. A machine-gun power-up gives unlimited ammo and faster fire for 10 s. Dynamite damages\neverything on screen, friend and foe. A damage bar fills when you are hit or shoot civilians or hostages. Clearing the\nVillage restores a lot of damage, clearing the Powder Magazine refills ammo and grenades, and the other stages restore a\nlittle. After the jungle the hero interrogates a soldier, who gives up the camp's location. In the camp you protect\nhostages as they run, and at the airport they run for the plane, with a bonus per hostage aboard. Big machine gunners die\nfast to shots in the face, and mortar crews sit behind sandbags. Enemy projectiles can be shot down. The game ends when the\ndamage bar fills or when both ammo and grenades run out. After the ending it loops at a higher difficulty.\n\n| We keep (what makes the source itself) | We change (to make it original, modern and fair) |\n|---|---|\n| A crosshair over a battlefield scrolling past in depth | An original setting, cast and stage names (Brimstone Coast, Hyena Legion, Wolfpack) |\n| A full-auto SMG with limited magazines; grenades with a big blast | A boss at the end of every operation, with weak points and phases |\n| A damage meter that fills; damage repaired on stage clears | A closing **telegraph ring** before every enemy shot, so damage is readable and avoidable |\n| Enemy quota counters per stage | Co-op for 1–4, locally and in AI Hub sessions |\n| Supplies from crates, barrels and animals | Animals are never killed: a hit makes them drop what they carry and flee |\n| A 10 s machine-gun power-up (**FURY**); **DYNAMITE** that hurts everyone on screen | No child civilians; a green cross, not the Red Cross emblem; no blood (dust, flashes, falls) |\n| Civilian and hostage penalties; hostage escorts in the camp and at the airfield | A streak multiplier, aim assist for pads, three difficulty levels |\n| Six stages: comms → jungle → village (heal) → ammo dump (refill) → camp → airport | Out of ammo and grenades ends the run only on ARCADE difficulty; on the others an emergency drop comes |\n| The interrogation beat; endings that depend on hostages saved; loop 2 after the ending | First-person SMG overlay, shell casings, rumble, a gibberish-voiced radio |\n\n---\n\n## 4. Genre, perspective, rendering approach\n\n- **Genre:** light-gun rail shooter, the shooting-gallery kind (catalogue genre: *Shooter*).\n- **Perspective:** first person. The camera sits on a scripted rail with a fixed heading per segment, at eye height. The\n  crosshair is free; the camera never follows it.\n- **Rendering:** the engine's **Mode 7 module** (`E.Mode7`, part of the full engine `my-3d2dge.js`): a perspective floor\n  drawn a row at a time, a `E.Backdrop` sky, and billboards drawn far to near at the size their distance gives. That means\n  HD Humanoid rigs (`m7.rig`), shapes-as-data (`m7.shape`: vehicles, buildings and props seen from the right side and\n  facing), string sprites (`m7.sprite`), and our own drawing (`m7.add(x, y, z, (g, sx, sy, k) => …)`, where `k` is screen\n  pixels per world unit there). This is the API card's light-gun recipe on the engine's own depth view (the 3D engine is not\n  in the handoff), and it gives real parallax: the camera moves sideways, and near things pass faster than far ones.\n- **Resolution:** `res: [480, 270]`, a fixed 16:9 buffer with **no `expand`**. Every player, screen and test then shares\n  one aim space; a 1080p TV is exactly ×4, and 4K is ×8. Leave `game.settings.scale` on `'auto'` (sharp on desktops;\n  players can pick fill). The page's bars use a CSS background: dark olive with a faint repeating wolf emblem, like\n  cabinet side art.\n- **Coordinates:** world units, 16 = 1 m; x east, y south, z up. Most rails run along +x at y = 0 with the camera looking\n  north (`angle = -Math.PI / 2`); depth ahead means negative y. Camera eye `h` is 22–28, `pitch` 4–8°, and `fov` 60°\n  (vertical). At 480×270 with fov 60, the focal length is `f = 135 / tan(30°) ≈ 234 px`. A rig 34 units tall stands about\n  130 px high at depth 60 (a foreground threat), 20 px at 400, and 9 px at 900. The visible half-width at depth D is about\n  1.03 × D.\n\n---\n\n## 5. Controls and prompts\n\nThe action map is a custom preset, `WP.INPUT`, passed to `new E.Game({ input })`; the menu actions come from the engine's\nown menu keys. Every action except the menu ones can be rebound on the standard Controls screen.\n\n| Action | DualSense (default glyphs) | Xbox glyphs (setting) | Keyboard + mouse | Keyboard only |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Aim | Left stick (fast) + right stick (half speed, fine), d-pad nudges | same | Mouse (absolute, like a light gun) | WASD / arrow keys |\n| Fire (hold = full-auto) | R2 (`Pad7`) | RT | Left click (`Mouse0`) | Space or J |\n| Grenade (thrown at the crosshair) | L2 (`Pad6`) or R1 (`Pad5`) | LT / RB | Right click (`Mouse2`) or G | K or G |\n| Reload | □ (`Pad2`) | X | R or middle click (`Mouse1`) | R or L |\n| Snap to threat (aim assist) | L1 (`Pad4`) | LB | Q | Q |\n| Pause / menu | Options (`Pad9`) | Menu | Esc or P | Esc or P |\n| Menu confirm / back | ✕ / ○ | A / B | Click / Esc, Enter / Backspace | Enter / Esc |\n| Join (local co-op) | Options on a controller nobody holds | Menu | n/a | n/a |\n\n```js\nWP.INPUT = {\n  up: ['KeyW', 'ArrowUp', 'Pad12'], down: ['KeyS', 'ArrowDown', 'Pad13'], left: ['KeyA', 'ArrowLeft', 'Pad14'], right: ['KeyD', 'ArrowRight', 'Pad15'],\n  fire: ['Mouse0', 'Space', 'KeyJ', 'Pad7'], grenade: ['Mouse2', 'KeyG', 'KeyK', 'Pad6', 'Pad5'],\n  reload: ['KeyR', 'KeyL', 'Mouse1', 'Pad2'], snap: ['KeyQ', 'Pad4'],\n  start: ['Enter', 'NumpadEnter', 'Pad9'], pause: ['Escape', 'KeyP', 'Pad9'],\n  confirm: ['Enter', 'NumpadEnter', 'Space', 'KeyJ', 'Pad0'], cancel: ['Escape', 'Backspace', 'Pad1']\n};\n```\n\n- **The crosshair from keys:** read the movement keys from `input.held` (`KeyW`, `KeyA`, `KeyS`, `KeyD`, `Arrow*`), not\n  from the `up`/`down`/`left`/`right` actions. The engine presses those actions when a stick passes halfway, which would\n  count the stick twice.\n- **Mouse:** the crosshair is `input.pointer()`, in the buffer's own pixels. Hide the OS cursor over the canvas in play\n  (`canvas.style.cursor = 'none'`) and show it in menus. In menus the mouse hovers to move the focus and clicks to pick\n  (`E.Menu` and `E.Shell` do both). On the map, hovering an operation node focuses it.\n- **Prompts** always come from `input.label(action)`, which follows `input.lastDevice`: \"R2\" / \"RT\" on a pad, \"Left\n  click\" after mouse use, \"Space\" after keyboard use. HUD prompts are short, glyph first: `□ RELOAD`, `L2 GRENADE`.\n- **Glyphs:** PlayStation by default, with an Xbox option. Change the engine's standard `glyphs` setting (see 6.5) so its\n  default is `'playstation'` and its options are PlayStation and Xbox only, and add a mirror in Settings → Game\n  (\"BUTTON SYMBOLS\") that calls `settings.set('glyphs', v)`. The engine's own entry lives on the Controls screen; the task\n  asks for it in Settings.\n- **Rumble** (`input.rumble`, per player's pad): each shot .06 strength for .03 s; your grenade .45 for .2 s; taking\n  damage .8 for .25 s; a boss explosion 1 for .6 s.\n- **Hub sessions:** ignore Options while Create (`Pad8`) is held. Create + Options belongs to the hub.\n\n---\n\n## 6. Architecture and modules\n\n### 6.1 Folder layout (inside the dev folder)\n\n```\npackage.json         scripts: test, build, check; devDependency playwright 1.63.0 (the handoff's pin)\nmanifest.json        the script order (engine first, then src/*) used by dev/index.html, the tests and the bundler\nengine/              the handoff copy: my-3d2dge.js (the full engine, edited), modules/, tools/, docs/, API.md, API-INDEX.md, AI_GUIDE.md\nengine/CHANGES.md    every engine edit and why (it feeds the Engine Feedback answers)\nhub/                 session.js (client 1.4.0, never edited), harness.html, bridge.js, round.js (harness files: tests only)\nsrc/                 the game: plain scripts sharing one namespace, WP (list below)\ndev/index.html       loads hub/session.js, engine/my-3d2dge.js and src/* one by one (works over file://)\ntools/build.mjs      bundles everything into ../OperationWolfpack-OperationWolf-claude-opus-5-5-max-20261103-174746.html\ntests/               run.mjs (the one command) and the suites (section 17)\nshots/               screenshots and filmstrips for visual review only (never bundled)\nvendor/              the clone the engine was copied from (not bundled)\n```\n\n### 6.2 Source files (`src/`, loaded in this order)\n\nEvery file starts `(() => { const E = My3D2dge, WP = globalThis.WP; ... })();` and adds to `WP`. No ES modules: the\nheadless tests load the files into a `node:vm` context, as the handoff's `game/test.mjs` does.\n\n| File | Owns |\n|---|---|\n| `00-core.js` | `globalThis.WP = {}`; constants (`W = 480`, `H = 270`, `PLAY_H = 234`); seat colors; small utils; the event bus |\n| `01-tuning.js` | `WP.T` (live tuning values) and `WP.TUNING_SPEC` (defaults, ranges, labels: 11.1); difficulty presets; co-op scaling |\n| `02-text.js` | `WP.TEXT`: every visible string (cards, radio, cutscenes, endings, HUD words, help, About). Nothing hard-coded elsewhere |\n| `03-rng.js` | seeded streams from `E.rng`: `sim`, `loot`, `ai`, `fx`; hub seeding |\n| `10-view3d.js` | `WP.View3D`: Mode 7 math for the sim (`begin`, `project`, `toFloor`, `halfWidth(depth)`), camera basis, camera-anchored → world |\n| `11-rail.js` | `WP.Rail`: keyframes → `E.Path` (catmull), speed, holds, tweens of yaw/pitch/h/fov, bob, segment cuts |\n| `12-hit.js` | hit shapes per entity in screen space, tests nearest first, cover occlusion, explosion queries |\n| `20-entities.js` | the entity store (ids, kinds), the `WP.TYPES` registry, shared update (anchors, timers, death, fade, despawn) |\n| `21-infantry.js` | infantry behaviors (7.6) |\n| `22-enemyfire.js` | telegraphs, target choice, hitscan accuracy, damage to players |\n| `23-projectiles.js` | grenades, knives, rockets, missiles, mortar and tank shells, flame waves: motion, shoot-down, impact |\n| `24-vehicles.js` | jeep, truck, APC, tank, skiff, gunboat, helicopter, cargo plane (scenery), weak points, wrecks |\n| `25-bosses.js` | the six bosses (8.7): phases, weak points, attacks, intros, defeats |\n| `26-friendlies.js` | civilians, hostages (escort, capture, recapture), supply animals, friendly markers |\n| `27-props.js` | destructibles and cover: crates, red barrels (chains), sandbags, masts, fuel tanks, searchlights, ropes, ammo wagons, window and door openings |\n| `28-pickups.js` | pickup kinds, drops, bounce and expiry, collection, the supply director, the emergency drop |\n| `30-players.js` | player state, device adapters → `InputFrame`, crosshair motion, aim assist, snap, join, down, return at the next op |\n| `31-weapon.js` | SMG (rate, bloom, magazines, reload, FURY), grenade throws, dynamite, shot resolution → damage, score, events |\n| `32-scoring.js` | scores, streaks, accuracy, the stage tally, ranks, high scores (through `E.Arcade`) |\n| `33-sim.js` | `WP.Sim`: one operation's run. `step(dt, frames)`, beats and spawns, quotas, reinforcements, boss trigger, clear or fail, `events`, `snapshot()` |\n| `34-mirror.js` | `WP.Mirror`: a hub client's world, rebuilt from snapshots and events, with the same read interface as `WP.Sim` |\n| `40-stages-common.js` | layout helpers (rows of palms, fence runs, sandbag lines, hut rows, tree walls), floor painters, sky specs, beat macros |\n| `41-op1.js` … `46-op6.js` | the stage data, one operation per file (8.6) |\n| `47-cutscenes.js` | briefing, interrogation, Kennel aftermath, takeoff cut, ending variants, as scripts |\n| `50-art-shapes.js` | new `E.SHAPES` entries, materials and patterns (9.1) |\n| `51-art-rigs.js` | Humanoid and Blob presets per type and cast member, the rig pool, custom poses (9.2) |\n| `52-art-sprites.js` | string sprites (pickups, projectiles, icons, emblems) and procedural sprites (muzzle flashes, explosion frames) |\n| `53-fx.js` | world FX particles (drawn through `m7.add`), floor decals; screen FX (grade, darkness mask, rain, vignette, flashes, hit markers, floating numbers, threat arrows) |\n| `54-render.js` | the frame: gather scenery (culled), entities and FX → `m7.draw`; post; telegraph rings; crosshairs |\n| `55-gun.js` | the first-person SMG overlay: model, recoil, reload, FURY glow, grenade hand |\n| `60-audio.js` | sound definitions, loops (rotors, engines, rain), the mixer, the rumble map |\n| `61-music.js` | songs as data; the music director (layers by intensity, ducking under radio lines) |\n| `62-voice.js` | the gibberish voice synth, speaker profiles, barks, the howl, radio crackle; the Dialog hook |\n| `70-hud.js` | the canvas HUD (10.3) |\n| `71-screens.js` | canvas screens: title, squad, map, briefing, op card, tally, continue, ending, credits, initials, About, the touch notice, the hub card |\n| `72-shell.js` | the `E.Shell` theme, 10-foot CSS, pause items, game settings, help |\n| `73-devmode.js` | the dev screen and the dev overlay (section 11) |\n| `80-bot.js` | `WP.Bot`, the AI player (attract mode, autopilot, tests) |\n| `81-attract.js` | the attract playlist and controller (`E.Arcade` phases) |\n| `90-net.js` | the AI Hub session: roles, card, start, inputs, snapshots, events, interpolation, finish (13.2) |\n| `91-codec.js` | compact encoding, the size guard, the rate limiter |\n| `95-scenes.js` | the scene table, flow and deep links (6.4) |\n| `99-main.js` | `new E.Game`, boot steps, settings, the debug handle, start |\n\n### 6.3 Layers and the one rule that keeps them testable\n\n- **The Sim** (`WP.Sim` and `20`–`33`) holds all game state and rules. It is deterministic: fixed steps, its own seeded\n  streams, no `Math.random`, DOM, audio or rigs. Each step it takes one `InputFrame` per player:\n  `{ x, y (crosshair in buffer px, floats), fire (held), grenade, reload, snap (presses this step), device }`. Every\n  noteworthy thing becomes an event in `sim.events` (`{ k: 'kill', id, by, part }`, `shot`, `hit`, `pickup`, `damage`,\n  `explode`, `spawn`, `telegraph`, `radio`, `boss`, `clear`, …).\n- **Presentation** (`50`–`72`) reads a world (a `WP.Sim`, or a `WP.Mirror` on a hub client) and consumes its events for\n  sound, FX, rig poses and the HUD. Rig posing happens in `update` (the engine's rule: draw only draws), but it is\n  cosmetic and never feeds back into the Sim.\n- **Players** (`30`) turn devices, the bot or the network into `InputFrame`s. Tests can inject frames directly:\n  `WP.Players.override(pid, fn)`.\n- **Net** (`90`, `91`) moves `InputFrame`s from clients to the host, and snapshots and events from the host to clients.\n\nHit tests run inside the Sim, on the Sim's own `WP.View3D` (a second `E.Mode7` used only for its math:\n`m7.begin({ W: 480, H: 270 }, cam)`, then `project` and `toFloor`). It is built from the same camera as the renderer's\n`E.Mode7`, so what you see is what you hit, headless included.\n\n### 6.4 Scenes, flow, deep links\n\nScenes (`game.start({ scenes, scene: 'boot', boot: { steps } })`):\n`boot` → `hubcard` (sessions only) or `title` ⇄ `attract` (demo, then the high-score table) → `squad` (optional co-op lobby) →\n`briefing` → `map` → `opcard` → `play` (`pausable: true`, except in sessions) → `tally` → (`cutscene`) → `map` → … →\n`ending` → `credits` → `initials` → `title`. Also `continue` (game over), `about`, `range` (dev target practice), and\n`touch` (the touch-only notice, laid over `title`).\n\nDeep links, for the tools and tests (read from `location.hash` at boot): `#title`, `#attract`, `#about`, `#map`, `#op1` …\n`#op6`, `#op3/boss`, `#op6/b` (the takeoff segment), `#cut/interrogation`, `#ending/0` … `#ending/5`, `#range`, `#dev`\n(play with the dev screen open).\n\n### 6.5 Engine usage and engine changes\n\nWhat the game uses from the engine: `E.Game` (fixed 1/120 s steps, scenes, fades, `flash`/`shake`/`freeze`/`timeScale`,\n`script`/`tween`/`after`/`every`, `advance` and `debug` for tests), `E.Mode7`, `E.Backdrop`, `E.Humanoid` and `E.MOVES`,\n`E.Blob`, `E.SHAPES` with `E.MATERIALS` and `E.PATTERNS` (`docs/ASSETS.md`), `E.sprite`/`E.anim`/`E.recolor`, `E.px.*`\nincluding `px.blend`, `px.ddisc` and `px.polyDither`, `E.font`, `E.ui`, `E.Dialog` (portraits, non-modal radio), `E.Menu`,\n`E.Shell` (pause, settings, controls, help, the dev screen), `game.settings` (`define`, `set`, `reset`), `game.input`\n(`pressed`, `down`, `held`, `pointer`, `padMove`, `padAim`, `label`, `glyphs`, `lastDevice`, `rumble`, and `tap`/`hold`/\n`feed` for tests), `game.allowJoin`/`addPlayer`/`players`, `E.Arcade` (cards, tables, initials, continue, attract phases),\n`game.audio` (`sfx`, `define`, `music` with tracks and layers, `duck`, `sting`), `E.rng`, `E.noise2`, `E.tex.*`, `E.arc`,\n`E.Path`, `E.store`, `E.versionLabel`. Look names up in `API-INDEX.md` (grep it; don't read it whole); `API.md` is the\ncard to read whole.\n\nEngine edits, each logged in `engine/CHANGES.md`:\n\n1. **Dialog voice as a function.** At the per-letter blip in `Dialog.update` (around line 8956 of v0.85.2:\n   `if (this.voice && …) this.game.audio.sfx(this.voice)`), when `typeof this.voice === 'function'`, call\n   `this.voice(ch, index, this)` instead. That is the hook for gibberish voices.\n2. **Shell rows screen.** `shell.show(name, spec)` gets `spec.rows`, a list of `[heading, [settingKeys]]` drawn with the\n   existing `_row(key)` (sliders, toggles, selects, with pad and keyboard focus), plus `spec.actions` buttons. The\n   standard Settings screen skips setting groups listed in `E.Shell.hiddenGroups` (set it to `['Dev']`).\n3. **A non-pausing menu for sessions.** `new E.Game({ menu: { pauses: false } })`: the pause and settings screens open\n   over a running game, which keeps calling `update`. The game's input is held back while a screen is open, as it is now.\n4. **Glyph setting.** The standard `glyphs` setting (around line 2999) defaults to `'playstation'`, with options\n   `[['playstation', 'PlayStation'], ['xbox', 'Xbox']]`.\n5. **Stereo pan (optional, nice to have).** `audio.sfx(voice, { pan: -1..1 })` through a `StereoPannerNode`, to place\n   gunfire, rotors and explosions left and right.\n6. **Anything else you find missing:** extend it in the engine and log it. Don't work around a bug in game code without\n   logging it either.\n\n`My3D2dge` loads headless in Node (no DOM). The game must too: guard every DOM use (`typeof document !== 'undefined'`),\nand keep the Sim free of it.\n\n### 6.6 Data formats\n\n**Stage definition** (one per `src/4x-opN.js`, pushed onto `WP.STAGES`):\n\n```js\nWP.STAGES.push({\n  id: 'op1', n: 1, name: 'RADIO HILL', card: 'DESTROY THE ENEMY RADIO STATION', music: 'op1', time: 'dawn',\n  sky: { sky: ['#1d1a3a', '#7a3f6e', '#e88a6a', '#f6c48c'], sun: { x: .7, y: .62, r: 18, color: '#ffd6a0' },\n         layers: [{ kind: 'mountains', color: '#5a3a5e', y: .58, height: .12, parallax: .1 }, { kind: 'sea', color: '#8a5a7a', y: .66 }] },\n  fog: { color: '#e0a07c', near: 320, far: 1600 }, grade: { mul: '#ffe6d6', add: '#100008' },\n  floor: 'hill',                        // a painter in WP.FLOORS: (x, y, t) => [r, g, b]\n  cam: { h: 28, pitch: 6, fov: 60 },\n  rail: [{ d: 0, x: 0, y: 0, angle: -Math.PI / 2 }, { d: 2700, x: 2700, y: 0 }],   // knots: position and tweened angle/h/pitch/fov\n  speed: 16,                            // units a second, times WP.T.railSpeed\n  holds: [{ d: 520, until: 'beat:b6', max: 15 }, { d: 1720, until: 'air', max: 30 }, { d: 2700, until: 'boss' }],\n  scenery: [['shape', 'radioMast', 900, -380, 0, { facing: 0 }], ['row', 'palm', 0, 2700, -900, { every: 60, jitter: 20 }]],\n  props: [['sandbags', 'sbA', 160, -110], ['crate', 'c1', 420, -120, { drop: 'mag' }], ['mast', 'm1', 900, -380]],\n  beats: [{ id: 'b1', at: 0, radio: 'op1.go' },\n          { id: 'b2', at: 40, spawn: [['rifleman', { side: 'R', dz: 200, n: 3, gap: .8, path: 'cross' }]] }],   // ids follow 8.6's table order\n  quotas: { soldier: 36, vehicle: 3, air: 1 }, objective: { kind: 'masts', ids: ['m1', 'm2', 'm3'] },\n  boss: 'bigEar', perk: 'commsDown', clearHeal: 10, animals: ['goat'],\n  attract: [{ from: 'b4', seconds: 25 }]  // the attract mode's segment of this op\n});\n```\n\n- Spawn positions are given **in the camera's frame at the moment the beat fires**: `lx` (sideways, + right), `dz` (depth\n  ahead), `z`. `side: 'L' | 'R'` puts the spawn just off that edge: `lx = ∓(halfWidth(dz) + 30)`. They become world\n  positions at once, so they parallax, unless `anchor: 'cam'`, which keeps them in the camera's frame (river pursuers, the\n  takeoff chase, the gunship).\n- Paths: a named behavior (`'cross'`, `'popup'`, …) or a list of `[lx, dz]` waypoints in the same frame. Cover references\n  name a prop id.\n- Beats fire when the rail passes `at`, or `after: 'b5'` (that beat is cleared), plus an optional `delay`.\n\n**Type registry** (`WP.TYPES[id]`): `{ kind: 'infantry' | 'vehicle' | 'air' | 'boat' | 'boss' | 'civilian' | 'hostage' |\n'animal' | 'projectile' | 'pickup' | 'prop', quota: 'soldier' | 'vehicle' | 'air' | 'boat' | null, hp, score, h (height\nin units), rig | shape | sprite, behavior, fire: { telegraph, bullets, accuracy, damage } | throws: {...}, weak: [...] }`.\nThe bestiary tables in section 8 are the source of these values.\n\n**Text:** `WP.TEXT['op1.go'] = { who: 'DEN', text: 'OPERATION WOLFPACK IS GO. GOOD HUNTING.' }`. Speakers map to voice\nprofiles and portraits.\n\n**Designer tools.** Stage data are plain arrays, and the dev screen can print a ready-to-paste spawn or prop line for the\npoint under the crosshair (section 11). New operations are new data files; new enemies are new `WP.TYPES` entries with a\nbehavior. That is what lets the rest of the game be built out later with normal designer tools.\n\n### 6.7 The debug handle (for the tests and the engine's tools)\n\n```js\ngame.debug = {\n  snapshot: () => ({ scene, op, beat, d, hold, quotas, boss: { id, phase, hp }, players: [{ pid, x, y, damage, mag, spares, grenades, score, streak, down }],\n                     counts: { enemies, projectiles, pickups, civilians, hostages }, hostagesSaved, events: lastN, dev: changedKeys, errors: game.errors.length }),\n  jump: (op, beatOrD) => {}, cheat: (name, on) => {}, tune: (key, value) => {}, spawn: (type, opts) => id,\n  aim: (pid, x, y) => {}, frames: (pid, fn) => {}, sim: () => currentSim, hub: () => WP.Net.state\n};\n```\n\n---\n\n## 7. Systems (exact rules and numbers)\n\nNumbers are VETERAN defaults. The live ones live in `WP.T`; section 11 lists the sliders. The difficulty and co-op\nmultipliers of 7.16 apply on top.\n\n### 7.1 Rail camera (`11-rail.js`)","numLines":400,"startLine":1,"totalLines":1519}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01H5WsuouLCPCpVBGRiEwBLq","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
