\",\"request_id\":\"req_011Cgh2hpt3QHhAoXSSVXXzp\",\"thinking_duration_ms\":8480,\"thinking_display\":\"updates\"}","+{\"type\":\"assistant\",\"message\":{\"model\":\"claude-opus-5-5\",\"id\":\"msg_011Cgh2hqMKfKocBzPrpKrrE\",\"type\":\"message\",\"role\":\"assistant\",\"content\":[{\"type\":\"tool_use\",\"id\":\"toolu_01XasYrdiy6AapnkcGmdp5VH\",\"name\":\"Read\",\"input\":{\"file_path\":\"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-max.plan__20261103-174749__x8de669/work/PLAN.md\",\"offset\":598,\"limit\":200},\"caller\":{\"type\":\"direct\"}}],\"container\":null,\"stop_reason\":null,\"stop_sequence\":null,\"stop_details\":null,\"usage\":{\"input_tokens\":2,\"cache_creation_input_tokens\":7879,\"cache_read_input_tokens\":495694,\"cache_creation\":{\"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens\":0,\"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens\":7879},\"output_tokens\":7,\"service_tier\":\"standard\",\"inference_geo\":\"global\",\"speed\":\"standard\"},\"input_transformations\":[],\"diagnostics\":null,\"context_management\":null},\"parent_tool_use_id\":null,\"session_id\":\"99d4115e-ac85-4844-bf9c-39009e8f9714\",\"uuid\":\"8805b44b-f3b8-48fe-8ab0-17fc7817eb35\",\"timestamp\":\"2026-11-04T01:36:10.285Z\",\"request_id\":\"req_011Cgh2hpt3QHhAoXSSVXXzp\",\"wire_tool_inputs\":{\"toolu_01XasYrdiy6AapnkcGmdp5VH\":{\"file_path\":\"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-max.plan__20261103-174749__x8de669/work/PLAN.md\",\"offset\":598,\"limit\":200}}}","+{\"type\":\"user\",\"message\":{\"role\":\"user\",\"content\":[{\"tool_use_id\":\"toolu_01XasYrdiy6AapnkcGmdp5VH\",\"type\":\"tool_result\",\"content\":\"598\\t  touch 0.\\n599\\t- **contact height h:** bump 18; set 40; attack = that player's reach, about `50·size/1.25` plus her jump apex\\n600\\t  `12 + 1.2·JMP` (≈ 58–66). If the ball is too low to set or attack, fall back to bump height.\\n601\\t- **t\\\\*** = `timeAtHeight(h)`. If the ball never comes down to h, use tLand − .05 and the ball's height then: a dig.\\n602\\t- **P\\\\*** = `posAt(t*)` on the ground, and her travel time to it.\\n603\\t\\n604\\t**Assignment.**\\n605\\t\\n606\\t- A human's call wins: Mine gives her the ball and Yours gives it to the partner, provided that player can reach P* by\\n607\\t  t* + .1 s.\\n608\\t- Otherwise the faster arrival takes it; on a tie, whoever didn't make the previous touch.\\n609\\t- The partner **covers** a ball assigned to the human only when the human is more than 40 units from P* at t* − .4 s.\\n610\\t\\n611\\t**Timing ring.** A ground ring at P* (decal) shrinks from radius 22 to the reach radius over the last .8 s. It turns\\n612\\tfrom white to gold inside the PERFECT window, and a \\\"!\\\" pip appears over the head .35 s before t*. Settings can turn it\\n613\\toff, and the final's Golden Glare hides it (§4.8.6).\\n614\\t\\n615\\t**The judge.**\\n616\\t\\n617\\t- The **first** `play` press in [t* − .6, t* + okWin] counts; other presses do nothing. err = tPress − t*.\\n618\\t- PERFECT if |err| ≤ `T.perfectWin` (.05 s), GREAT if ≤ `T.greatWin` (.10), OK if ≤ `T.okWin` (.16); otherwise MISS:\\n619\\t  a whiff animation and a .45 s lock.\\n620\\t- **Reach check:** horizontal distance from the player to P* ≤ `T.reach` (14 + .4·TEC) keeps the judged quality; up to\\n621\\t  8 more gives a **stretch** (one quality lower); beyond that, a whiff.\\n622\\t- **Timing assist** multiplies the windows: Tight × .8, Normal × 1, Generous × 1.6.\\n623\\t- **Auto-receive** (Settings; forced on for the tutorial's first 2 receives): with no press by t*, a receive (not a set\\n624\\t  or spike) happens at OK quality.\\n625\\t- No press means the ball passes her. If it lands, the point is lost, unless the partner covered it (see Assignment\\n626\\t  above).\\n627\\t\\n628\\t**What each quality does:**\\n629\\t\\n630\\t| Quality | Accuracy radius around the intended target | Speed |\\n631\\t| --- | --- | --- |\\n632\\t| PERFECT | 4 | × 1.12 |\\n633\\t| GREAT | 10 | × 1.0 |\\n634\\t| OK | 20 | × .85 |\\n635\\t| stretch | 30 | × .8 |\\n636\\t\\n637\\t**Actions:**\\n638\\t\\n639\\t| Action | Input | What happens |\\n640\\t| --- | --- | --- |\\n641\\t| **Receive / dig** (touch 0) | Play | Bump to the **pass target** (NET_X ∓ 40, the partner's y) in passT; the stick biases the target ±24. Holding **Shot** instead sends a free ball over the net to the deepest open zone. |\\n642\\t| **Set** (touch 1) | Play | Set to the **attack spot** (NET_X ∓ 22, the hitter's y) in setT. Stick neutral = high set; toward the net = quick set (T .75, lower apex); away = back set. **Shot** = setter dump (a tip to the nearest open short zone, T .6). |\\n643\\t| **Attack** (touch 2) | Play = spike, Shot = cut/roll shot | She approaches and jumps on her own: the jump starts at t* − jumpTime(JMP) so her hand meets the ball at t*. The press at t* swings. Spike speed is `T.spikeSpeed × (0.85 + .035·POW) × quality`; a shot takes shotT and peaks just over the block. If she wasn't in reach (a bad set), the press makes a high, soft free ball. |\\n644\\t| **Aim** | Stick at contact, or mouse | Pad and keyboard pick one of 9 zones on the opponents' half: neutral = deep centre, up/down on screen = line or cross-court, toward the net = short angle, away = deep. With the mouse as the last device, the reticle's ground point is the target, clamped 6 inside the lines. Quality then scatters by the radius above, which can push it out. |\\n645\\t| **Spike assist** | difficulty | If the aimed spike can't clear the net from the contact point, raise the arc just enough: always on Easy; on Normal only when the miss would be under 4 units; never on Hard or Pro, where it goes into the net. |\\n646\\t| **Serve** | Play (float) or Shot (jump serve) to toss; the same button again at the top of the toss | Before the toss she walks along the baseline with the stick. The hit's timing is judged against the toss apex with the same windows. Aim with the stick or mouse; a target marker shows during the toss. Jump serves are faster and scatter × 1.5. No second press within 1.2 s is a serve fault. AI serves after 1.2–2.5 s; a human gets an automatic float serve after 10 s. |\\n647\\t| **Block** | Shot, within 20 of the net while the opponents attack | She jumps; her hands reach the top `T.blockRise` (.28 s) after the press and stay up .18 s. When the spike crosses the net plane, it is blocked if abs(y_ball − y_blocker) ≤ 9 + .6·JMP and NET_H ≤ z_ball ≤ handTop(t). Timing error = (tPress + blockRise) − tNet. PERFECT or GREAT is a **stuff** (back down on the attacker's side: v = (−.35vx, .3vy ± 20, −60)). OK is a **soft block**: it pops up on her side as a touch. Cut shots arc over the block unless she has JMP 9 and timed it PERFECT. |\\n648\\t| **Dive** | Dive | Lunge toward the stick (or toward P* when neutral) at 190 u/s for .22 s, slide .3 s, down .45 s. Reach is +`T.diveReach` (16) during the lunge. A judged Play press during it is a **dig**: accuracy radius × 1.6 and a higher apex. A dive with no ball is just the lunge and recovery. |\\n649\\t| **Call** | Call | Cycles mine → yours → auto. A speech bubble (\\\"MINE!\\\" / \\\"YOURS!\\\") appears with a gibberish shout. AI teammates call too (\\\"MINE!\\\" when they take a ball). |\\n650\\t\\n651\\tEvery action sets the rig state `{ act, phase, u }` for animation (§4.26).\\n652\\t\\n653\\t### 4.7 AI (`23-ai.js`)\\n654\\t\\n655\\t**Skill.** Each AI player has `skill = clamp(base × (0.8 + 0.05 × relevant stat), 0, 1)`.\\n656\\t\\n657\\t- `base` is the day's value (§5.6) plus the difficulty offset (Easy −.2, Normal 0, Hard +.12, Pro +.22).\\n658\\t- An AI partner of the player also gets × chemistry: `(1 − T.chemistry/2) + T.chemistry × affinity/100` (default\\n659\\t  `T.chemistry` .3, which gives .85–1.15).\\n660\\t\\n661\\t**Reaction.** She starts toward P* `T.aiReaction × (1.2 − skill)` s after a launch, and moves at speed × `T.aiSpeedMul`.\\n662\\t\\n663\\t**Errors per touch.** Roll on the `match` rng:\\n664\\t\\n665\\t| Outcome | Probability |\\n666\\t| --- | --- |\\n667\\t| PERFECT | .10 + .45·skill |\\n668\\t| GREAT | .30 |\\n669\\t| MISS | max(.02, .16·(1 − skill)): a shank in a random direction, or no touch |\\n670\\t| OK | the rest |\\n671\\t\\n672\\t**Formation by phase:**\\n673\\t\\n674\\t| Phase | Positions |\\n675\\t| --- | --- |\\n676\\t| receiving serve | split the court in y, 40% deep |\\n677\\t| own attack | the setter under the pass target; the hitter backs off for her approach |\\n678\\t| defending an attack | if the set is near the net and a skill roll passes, one blocks in line with the attacker; the other defends deep cross-court |\\n679\\t| free ball | both back |\\n680\\t\\n681\\t**Decisions.**\\n682\\t\\n683\\t- **Attack target:** of the 9 zones, the one with the largest \\\"distance to the nearest defender's predicted spot minus\\n684\\t  her reach\\\", minus 8·(1 − skill) for zones within 10 of a line.\\n685\\t- **Shot or spike:** a shot when a blocker is up and in line, plus 20% at random for variety.\\n686\\t- **Serve:** float 70% and jump 30% (50% jump when POW ≥ 8), aimed at the deep corners or the seam between receivers.\\n687\\t- **Setter dump:** 8% when the opponents' front court is empty.\\n688\\t\\n689\\t**AI with a human teammate.** Beach pattern: whoever passes, attacks. When the human passes, the AI sets back to her;\\n690\\twhen the AI passes, the human sets and the AI attacks.\\n691\\t\\n692\\t**Spectacle mode** (attract only): perfect skill until the rally reaches a random 3–9 touches, then the attacking side's\\n693\\tspike goes into the biggest gap, a guaranteed kill.\\n694\\t\\n695\\t**Determinism.** Every roll comes from `G.rng('match')`, so the same seed and inputs give the same score (tested).\\n696\\t\\n697\\t### 4.8 Match flow and presentation (`24-match.js`)\\n698\\t\\n699\\t**4.8.1 Phases.** `intro` → `coin` → `serveSetup` → `toss` → `rally` → `dead` → `point` → [`replay`] → … →\\n700\\t`matchPoint` sting → `end`.\\n701\\t\\n702\\t- `intro`: walk-on, Sonny's PA line, the teams line up at the net; 3 s, skippable.\\n703\\t- `coin`: who serves; 1 s.\\n704\\t\\n705\\t**4.8.2 Cameras.**\\n706\\t\\n707\\t- **Broadcast:** `cam.frame([ball, ...players, netTop], { margin: 28, zoom: [.78, 1] })` with `cam.smooth` .22. During\\n708\\t  `serveSetup` it frames the server and the receivers.\\n709\\t- **Serve Cam:** follows the ball's x with a lead, fixed at the court's centre y, zoom .9.\\n710\\t- `view` (V / R3) toggles them; Settings has \\\"Match camera\\\". The scene's `camera: { zoom: [.85, 1.25] }` lets the\\n711\\t  player nudge the zoom.\\n712\\t\\n713\\t**4.8.3 HUD** (pixel art, anchored to the screen):\\n714\\t\\n715\\t| Where | What |\\n716\\t| --- | --- |\\n717\\t| top left | TV score bug: per team two small portraits, names, score, a serve ball icon, set count, and a MATCH POINT tag |\\n718\\t| top right | ClockBadge (vacation) or the hub clock |\\n719\\t| bottom left | partner card: portrait, name, hearts = chemistry, mood |\\n720\\t| bottom centre | context prompts (at most 3, e.g. \\\"✕ BUMP · ○ DIVE · △ CALL\\\") |\\n721\\t| at players | judgement popups (PERFECT! gold, GREAT cyan, OK white, MISS grey) and call bubbles |\\n722\\t| bottom right | commentary box: `E.Dialog` with `modal: false, auto: 2.2`, Sonny's portrait, his voice |\\n723\\t\\n724\\t**4.8.4 Crowd and set dressing.**\\n725\\t\\n726\\t- 24 spectators on the far-side bleachers and towels, drawn as small pixel sprites like the sports kit's crowd, plus 4\\n727\\t  HD rigs at size .9 drawn `every: 2`. They sway, jump and cheer on points (more for the home side) and groan at errors.\\n728\\t- Sonny reacts at his DJ booth.\\n729\\t- Pepper sits at the scorer's table beside a flip scoreboard shape whose cards change with the score.\\n730\\t- Night matches add floodlights (lights) and tiki torches.\\n731\\t\\n732\\t**4.8.5 Celebrations.**\\n733\\t\\n734\\t| Moment | Animation |\\n735\\t| --- | --- |\\n736\\t| point won | the partners high-five (§4.26), plus a fist pump |\\n737\\t| ace, kill or block | the scorer's signature celebration |\\n738\\t| point lost | a shrug, an encouraging pat or a facepalm (short) |\\n739\\t| match won | both cheer, then the MVP's signature move |\\n740\\t| match lost | kneel and sigh |\\n741\\t\\n742\\tAll of these fit inside the `point` phase.\\n743\\t\\n744\\t**4.8.6 Specials in the final against Sonny & Pepper (\\\"The House\\\").** Each is telegraphed in Pepper's Day 4 letter and\\n745\\tby the commentary.\\n746\\t\\n747\\t- **Golden Glare:** half of Sonny's serves. His sunglasses flash (a .6 s telegraph: glint particle, `sfx('glare')`), and\\n748\\t  the receiving human's timing ring is hidden for that serve (\\\"DAZZLED!\\\"). A receiver wearing **any sunglasses**\\n749\\t  accessory is immune (\\\"SHADES ON!\\\").\\n750\\t- **House Edge:** once per set Pepper hits a precise line shot. The AI partner covers it only at chemistry Good Friend\\n751\\t  or better (\\\"I've got line!\\\").\\n752\\t- **Lucky Seven:** the first time The House reaches game point, Sonny's next attack clips the tape and is guaranteed to\\n753\\t  dribble over.\\n754\\t\\n755\\t**4.8.7 Highlight replay.**\\n756\\t\\n757\\t- Keep a ring buffer of the last 6 s at 60 Hz: rig states, launches and camera.\\n758\\t- After an ace, a stuff block, a spike over 400 u/s, or a rally of 8+ touches (at most once every 3 points), replay the\\n759\\t  last 3 s at .4× from the other camera with a \\\"REPLAY\\\" letterbox. Any button skips.\\n760\\t- Off in hub sessions.\\n761\\t\\n762\\t**4.8.8 Results.**\\n763\\t\\n764\\t- The winners pose.\\n765\\t- Three big stat cards: points, kills, longest rally. The MVP (most points) gets a portrait and her signature move.\\n766\\t- Vacation: prize money counts up, the partner's affinity change animates as hearts, then **Continue**.\\n767\\t- Beach Match: Rematch / Change setup / Title.\\n768\\t\\n769\\t**4.8.9 Sound.**\\n770\\t\\n771\\t- The match song `spike` has a named drum layer `rally` that comes in on long rallies (`audio.layer`). At match point\\n772\\t  the tempo goes up by 6 (`audio.tempo`) with a sting.\\n773\\t- Ball sounds per kind, the whistle, crowd swells, and Sonny's PA voice.\\n774\\t\\n775\\t### 4.9 Beach Match: exhibition and local multiplayer (`52-exhibition.js`)\\n776\\t\\n777\\t**The setup screen.** Four columns, one per slot (Left 1, Left 2, Right 1, Right 2). Each shows a fighter on a pedestal\\n778\\tand who controls it: CPU or P1–P4.\\n779\\t\\n780\\t- **Joining:** `game.allowJoin({ max: 4, onJoin })`. Start or ✕ on a free pad, or 2 on the keyboard (the split halves).\\n781\\t- **Each joined player:** left/right moves her badge between slots; up/down picks the fighter; □ picks the outfit\\n782\\t  (signature suits, plus suits ever owned in a vacation, or every suit once a vacation is finished); ✕ is ready.\\n783\\t- **Options row:** points (7/11/15/21), sets (1/3), difficulty, court time (Morning/Sunset/Night), camera.\\n784\\t- The match starts when every joined player is ready.\\n785\\t\\n786\\t**Any mix works:** 1P with a CPU partner vs CPU; 2P co-op vs CPU; 1v1 with CPU partners; 2v2; 3 players (2 vs 1 + CPU).\\n787\\t\\n788\\tEach player's prompts use her own device (`p.input.label`, per-player glyph style). Results offer Rematch / Change setup\\n789\\t/ Title.\\n790\\t\\n791\\t### 4.10 Calendar, slots, weather, schedules, moods (`30-vacation.js`)\\n792\\t\\n793\\t**Days and slots.** `DAYS = 5` (data). Slots are Morning (06–12), Afternoon (12–18) and Night (18–24). The ClockBadge\\n794\\tshows \\\"Day 2 · Afternoon\\\" with weather.\\n795\\t\\n796\\t**What uses up a slot:** finishing a match; finishing Pool Hop; leaving a location you entered (the confirm says\\n797\\t\\\"Leaving ends the Morning.\\\"); leaving the casino at night; Rest in the villa. Talking, gifting, shopping and changing\"}]},\"parent_tool_use_id\":null,\"session_id\":\"99d4115e-ac85-4844-bf9c-39009e8f9714\",\"uuid\":\"e0ca4dba-47c7-46cd-b1f7-d60c4bd5ba20\",\"timestamp\":\"2026-11-04T01:36:10.291Z\",\"tool_use_result\":{\"type\":\"text\",\"file\":{\"filePath\":\"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-max.plan__20261103-174749__x8de669/work/PLAN.md\",\"content\":\"  touch 0.\\n- **contact height h:** bump 18; set 40; attack = that player's reach, about `50·size/1.25` plus her jump apex\\n  `12 + 1.2·JMP` (≈ 58–66). If the ball is too low to set or attack, fall back to bump height.\\n- **t\\\\*** = `timeAtHeight(h)`. If the ball never comes down to h, use tLand − .05 and the ball's height then: a dig.\\n- **P\\\\*** = `posAt(t*)` on the ground, and her travel time to it.\\n\\n**Assignment.**\\n\\n- A human's call wins: Mine gives her the ball and Yours gives it to the partner, provided that player can reach P* by\\n  t* + .1 s.\\n- Otherwise the faster arrival takes it; on a tie, whoever didn't make the previous touch.\\n- The partner **covers** a ball assigned to the human only when the human is more than 40 units from P* at t* − .4 s.\\n\\n**Timing ring.** A ground ring at P* (decal) shrinks from radius 22 to the reach radius over the last .8 s. It turns\\nfrom white to gold inside the PERFECT window, and a \\\"!\\\" pip appears over the head .35 s before t*. Settings can turn it\\noff, and the final's Golden Glare hides it (§4.8.6).\\n\\n**The judge.**\\n\\n- The **first** `play` press in [t* − .6, t* + okWin] counts; other presses do nothing. err = tPress − t*.\\n- PERFECT if |err| ≤ `T.perfectWin` (.05 s), GREAT if ≤ `T.greatWin` (.10), OK if ≤ `T.okWin` (.16); otherwise MISS:\\n  a whiff animation and a .45 s lock.\\n- **Reach check:** horizontal distance from the player to P* ≤ `T.reach` (14 + .4·TEC) keeps the judged quality; up to\\n  8 more gives a **stretch** (one quality lower); beyond that, a whiff.\\n- **Timing assist** multiplies the windows: Tight × .8, Normal × 1, Generous × 1.6.\\n- **Auto-receive** (Settings; forced on for the tutorial's first 2 receives): with no press by t*, a receive (not a set\\n  or spike) happens at OK quality.\\n- No press means the ball passes her. If it lands, the point is lost, unless the partner covered it (see Assignment\\n  above).\\n\\n**What each quality does:**\\n\\n| Quality | Accuracy radius around the intended target | Speed |\\n| --- | --- | --- |\\n| PERFECT | 4 | × 1.12 |\\n| GREAT | 10 | × 1.0 |\\n| OK | 20 | × .85 |\\n| stretch | 30 | × .8 |\\n\\n**Actions:**\\n\\n| Action | Input | What happens |\\n| --- | --- | --- |\\n| **Receive / dig** (touch 0) | Play | Bump to the **pass target** (NET_X ∓ 40, the partner's y) in passT; the stick biases the target ±24. Holding **Shot** instead sends a free ball over the net to the deepest open zone. |\\n| **Set** (touch 1) | Play | Set to the **attack spot** (NET_X ∓ 22, the hitter's y) in setT. Stick neutral = high set; toward the net = quick set (T .75, lower apex); away = back set. **Shot** = setter dump (a tip to the nearest open short zone, T .6). |\\n| **Attack** (touch 2) | Play = spike, Shot = cut/roll shot | She approaches and jumps on her own: the jump starts at t* − jumpTime(JMP) so her hand meets the ball at t*. The press at t* swings. Spike speed is `T.spikeSpeed × (0.85 + .035·POW) × quality`; a shot takes shotT and peaks just over the block. If she wasn't in reach (a bad set), the press makes a high, soft free ball. |\\n| **Aim** | Stick at contact, or mouse | Pad and keyboard pick one of 9 zones on the opponents' half: neutral = deep centre, up/down on screen = line or cross-court, toward the net = short angle, away = deep. With the mouse as the last device, the reticle's ground point is the target, clamped 6 inside the lines. Quality then scatters by the radius above, which can push it out. |\\n| **Spike assist** | difficulty | If the aimed spike can't clear the net from the contact point, raise the arc just enough: always on Easy; on Normal only when the miss would be under 4 units; never on Hard or Pro, where it goes into the net. |\\n| **Serve** | Play (float) or Shot (jump serve) to toss; the same button again at the top of the toss | Before the toss she walks along the baseline with the stick. The hit's timing is judged against the toss apex with the same windows. Aim with the stick or mouse; a target marker shows during the toss. Jump serves are faster and scatter × 1.5. No second press within 1.2 s is a serve fault. AI serves after 1.2–2.5 s; a human gets an automatic float serve after 10 s. |\\n| **Block** | Shot, within 20 of the net while the opponents attack | She jumps; her hands reach the top `T.blockRise` (.28 s) after the press and stay up .18 s. When the spike crosses the net plane, it is blocked if abs(y_ball − y_blocker) ≤ 9 + .6·JMP and NET_H ≤ z_ball ≤ handTop(t). Timing error = (tPress + blockRise) − tNet. PERFECT or GREAT is a **stuff** (back down on the attacker's side: v = (−.35vx, .3vy ± 20, −60)). OK is a **soft block**: it pops up on her side as a touch. Cut shots arc over the block unless she has JMP 9 and timed it PERFECT. |\\n| **Dive** | Dive | Lunge toward the stick (or toward P* when neutral) at 190 u/s for .22 s, slide .3 s, down .45 s. Reach is +`T.diveReach` (16) during the lunge. A judged Play press during it is a **dig**: accuracy radius × 1.6 and a higher apex. A dive with no ball is just the lunge and recovery. |\\n| **Call** | Call | Cycles mine → yours → auto. A speech bubble (\\\"MINE!\\\" / \\\"YOURS!\\\") appears with a gibberish shout. AI teammates call too (\\\"MINE!\\\" when they take a ball). |\\n\\nEvery action sets the rig state `{ act, phase, u }` for animation (§4.26).\\n\\n### 4.7 AI (`23-ai.js`)\\n\\n**Skill.** Each AI player has `skill = clamp(base × (0.8 + 0.05 × relevant stat), 0, 1)`.\\n\\n- `base` is the day's value (§5.6) plus the difficulty offset (Easy −.2, Normal 0, Hard +.12, Pro +.22).\\n- An AI partner of the player also gets × chemistry: `(1 − T.chemistry/2) + T.chemistry × affinity/100` (default\\n  `T.chemistry` .3, which gives .85–1.15).\\n\\n**Reaction.** She starts toward P* `T.aiReaction × (1.2 − skill)` s after a launch, and moves at speed × `T.aiSpeedMul`.\\n\\n**Errors per touch.** Roll on the `match` rng:\\n\\n| Outcome | Probability |\\n| --- | --- |\\n| PERFECT | .10 + .45·skill |\\n| GREAT | .30 |\\n| MISS | max(.02, .16·(1 − skill)): a shank in a random direction, or no touch |\\n| OK | the rest |\\n\\n**Formation by phase:**\\n\\n| Phase | Positions |\\n| --- | --- |\\n| receiving serve | split the court in y, 40% deep |\\n| own attack | the setter under the pass target; the hitter backs off for her approach |\\n| defending an attack | if the set is near the net and a skill roll passes, one blocks in line with the attacker; the other defends deep cross-court |\\n| free ball | both back |\\n\\n**Decisions.**\\n\\n- **Attack target:** of the 9 zones, the one with the largest \\\"distance to the nearest defender's predicted spot minus\\n  her reach\\\", minus 8·(1 − skill) for zones within 10 of a line.\\n- **Shot or spike:** a shot when a blocker is up and in line, plus 20% at random for variety.\\n- **Serve:** float 70% and jump 30% (50% jump when POW ≥ 8), aimed at the deep corners or the seam between receivers.\\n- **Setter dump:** 8% when the opponents' front court is empty.\\n\\n**AI with a human teammate.** Beach pattern: whoever passes, attacks. When the human passes, the AI sets back to her;\\nwhen the AI passes, the human sets and the AI attacks.\\n\\n**Spectacle mode** (attract only): perfect skill until the rally reaches a random 3–9 touches, then the attacking side's\\nspike goes into the biggest gap, a guaranteed kill.\\n\\n**Determinism.** Every roll comes from `G.rng('match')`, so the same seed and inputs give the same score (tested).\\n\\n### 4.8 Match flow and presentation (`24-match.js`)\\n\\n**4.8.1 Phases.** `intro` → `coin` → `serveSetup` → `toss` → `rally` → `dead` → `point` → [`replay`] → … →\\n`matchPoint` sting → `end`.\\n\\n- `intro`: walk-on, Sonny's PA line, the teams line up at the net; 3 s, skippable.\\n- `coin`: who serves; 1 s.\\n\\n**4.8.2 Cameras.**\\n\\n- **Broadcast:** `cam.frame([ball, ...players, netTop], { margin: 28, zoom: [.78, 1] })` with `cam.smooth` .22. During\\n  `serveSetup` it frames the server and the receivers.\\n- **Serve Cam:** follows the ball's x with a lead, fixed at the court's centre y, zoom .9.\\n- `view` (V / R3) toggles them; Settings has \\\"Match camera\\\". The scene's `camera: { zoom: [.85, 1.25] }` lets the\\n  player nudge the zoom.\\n\\n**4.8.3 HUD** (pixel art, anchored to the screen):\\n\\n| Where | What |\\n| --- | --- |\\n| top left | TV score bug: per team two small portraits, names, score, a serve ball icon, set count, and a MATCH POINT tag |\\n| top right | ClockBadge (vacation) or the hub clock |\\n| bottom left | partner card: portrait, name, hearts = chemistry, mood |\\n| bottom centre | context prompts (at most 3, e.g. \\\"✕ BUMP · ○ DIVE · △ CALL\\\") |\\n| at players | judgement popups (PERFECT! gold, GREAT cyan, OK white, MISS grey) and call bubbles |\\n| bottom right | commentary box: `E.Dialog` with `modal: false, auto: 2.2`, Sonny's portrait, his voice |\\n\\n**4.8.4 Crowd and set dressing.**\\n\\n- 24 spectators on the far-side bleachers and towels, drawn as small pixel sprites like the sports kit's crowd, plus 4\\n  HD rigs at size .9 drawn `every: 2`. They sway, jump and cheer on points (more for the home side) and groan at errors.\\n- Sonny reacts at his DJ booth.\\n- Pepper sits at the scorer's table beside a flip scoreboard shape whose cards change with the score.\\n- Night matches add floodlights (lights) and tiki torches.\\n\\n**4.8.5 Celebrations.**\\n\\n| Moment | Animation |\\n| --- | --- |\\n| point won | the partners high-five (§4.26), plus a fist pump |\\n| ace, kill or block | the scorer's signature celebration |\\n| point lost | a shrug, an encouraging pat or a facepalm (short) |\\n| match won | both cheer, then the MVP's signature move |\\n| match lost | kneel and sigh |\\n\\nAll of these fit inside the `point` phase.\\n\\n**4.8.6 Specials in the final against Sonny & Pepper (\\\"The House\\\").** Each is telegraphed in Pepper's Day 4 letter and\\nby the commentary.\\n\\n- **Golden Glare:** half of Sonny's serves. His sunglasses flash (a .6 s telegraph: glint particle, `sfx('glare')`), and\\n  the receiving human's timing ring is hidden for that serve (\\\"DAZZLED!\\\"). A receiver wearing **any sunglasses**\\n  accessory is immune (\\\"SHADES ON!\\\").\\n- **House Edge:** once per set Pepper hits a precise line shot. The AI partner covers it only at chemistry Good Friend\\n  or better (\\\"I've got line!\\\").\\n- **Lucky Seven:** the first time The House reaches game point, Sonny's next attack clips the tape and is guaranteed to\\n  dribble over.\\n\\n**4.8.7 Highlight replay.**\\n\\n- Keep a ring buffer of the last 6 s at 60 Hz: rig states, launches and camera.\\n- After an ace, a stuff block, a spike over 400 u/s, or a rally of 8+ touches (at most once every 3 points), replay the\\n  last 3 s at .4× from the other camera with a \\\"REPLAY\\\" letterbox. Any button skips.\\n- Off in hub sessions.\\n\\n**4.8.8 Results.**\\n\\n- The winners pose.\\n- Three big stat cards: points, kills, longest rally. The MVP (most points) gets a portrait and her signature move.\\n- Vacation: prize money counts up, the partner's affinity change animates as hearts, then **Continue**.\\n- Beach Match: Rematch / Change setup / Title.\\n\\n**4.8.9 Sound.**\\n\\n- The match song `spike` has a named drum layer `rally` that comes in on long rallies (`audio.layer`). At match point\\n  the tempo goes up by 6 (`audio.tempo`) with a sting.\\n- Ball sounds per kind, the whistle, crowd swells, and Sonny's PA voice.\\n\\n### 4.9 Beach Match: exhibition and local multiplayer (`52-exhibition.js`)\\n\\n**The setup screen.** Four columns, one per slot (Left 1, Left 2, Right 1, Right 2). Each shows a fighter on a pedestal\\nand who controls it: CPU or P1–P4.\\n\\n- **Joining:** `game.allowJoin({ max: 4, onJoin })`. Start or ✕ on a free pad, or 2 on the keyboard (the split halves).\\n- **Each joined player:** left/right moves her badge between slots; up/down picks the fighter; □ picks the outfit\\n  (signature suits, plus suits ever owned in a vacation, or every suit once a vacation is finished); ✕ is ready.\\n- **Options row:** points (7/11/15/21), sets (1/3), difficulty, court time (Morning/Sunset/Night), camera.\\n- The match starts when every joined player is ready.\\n\\n**Any mix works:** 1P with a CPU partner vs CPU; 2P co-op vs CPU; 1v1 with CPU partners; 2v2; 3 players (2 vs 1 + CPU).\\n\\nEach player's prompts use her own device (`p.input.label`, per-player glyph style). Results offer Rematch / Change setup\\n/ Title.\\n\\n### 4.10 Calendar, slots, weather, schedules, moods (`30-vacation.js`)\\n\\n**Days and slots.** `DAYS = 5` (data). Slots are Morning (06–12), Afternoon (12–18) and Night (18–24). The ClockBadge\\nshows \\\"Day 2 · Afternoon\\\" with weather.\\n\\n**What uses up a slot:** finishing a match; finishing Pool Hop; leaving a location you entered (the confirm says\\n\\\"Leaving ends the Morning.\\\"); leaving the casino at night; Rest in the villa. Talking, gifting, shopping and changing\",\"numLines\":200,\"startLine\":598,\"totalLines\":2180}},\"tool_result_meta\":[{\"id\":\"toolu_01XasYrdiy6AapnkcGmdp5VH\",\"permission_decision\":{\"decision\":\"accept\",\"source\":\"config\",\"reason_type\":\"mode\"}}]}"]}]},{"filePath":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-max.plan__20261103-174752__xfb4127/agent.out","hunks":[{"oldStart":1959,"oldLines":3,"newStart":1959,"newLines":5,"lines":[" {\"type\":\"system\",\"subtype\":\"thinking_tokens\",\"estimated_tokens\":150,\"estimated_tokens_delta\":100,\"session_id\":\"4e34a208-b0be-46a3-80b7-32b0075578cc\",\"uuid\":\"73705970-131e-4cf7-b2d0-313915d036a4\"}"," {\"type\":\"system\",\"subtype\":\"thinking_tokens\",\"estimated_tokens\":300,\"estimated_tokens_delta\":150,\"session_id\":\"4e34a208-b0be-46a3-80b7-32b0075578cc\",\"uuid\":\"c60cc3e5-caad-47d8-a182-2e652d74095f\"}"," {\"type\":\"system\",\"subtype\":\"thinking_tokens\",\"estimated_tokens\":400,\"estimated_tokens_delta\":100,\"session_id\":\"4e34a208-b0be-46a3-80b7-32b0075578cc\",\"uuid\":\"efc5f883-0785-438d-977d-52e67b0aebc3\"}","+{\"type\":\"system\",\"subtype\":\"thinking_tokens\",\"estimated_tokens\":500,\"estimated_tokens_delta\":100,\"session_id\":\"4e34a208-b0be-46a3-80b7-32b0075578cc\",\"uuid\":\"5b56c690-a76b-4f93-a61a-108d65061f23\"}","+{\"type\":\"system\",\"subtype\":\"thinking_tokens\",\"estimated_tokens\":650,\"estimated_tokens_delta\":150,\"session_id\":\"4e34a208-b0be-46a3-80b7-32b0075578cc\",\"uuid\":\"ba8f6d6e-46ef-4029-b19a-dc5c08b88c9d\"}"]}]}],"moreFiles":10,"changedFiles":["/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-haiku-5-5-medium.build__20261103-183312__xf5fb84/agent.out","/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-haiku-5-5-medium.build__20261103-183606__xf7f297/agent.out","/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-max.plan__20261103-174737__x193f88/agent.out","/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-max.plan__20261103-174740__x7ef5ea/agent.out","/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-max.plan__20261103-174749__x8de669/agent.out","/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-max.plan__20261103-174752__xfb4127/agent.out","/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-max.plan__20261103-174758__x42640e/agent.out","/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-max.plan__20261103-174807__x5c5240/agent.out","/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-max.plan__20261103-174819__xcfe199/agent.out","/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-max.plan__20261103-174822__x21985c/agent.out","/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-xhigh.build__20261103-182647__x9a2bbb/agent.out","/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-xhigh.build__20261103-183009__xe162c9/work/OperationWolfpack-OperationWolf-claude-opus-5-5-xhig