{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CghBMH7b732m6segGPRjd","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I built **Operation Wolfpack**, an original first-person light-gun rail shooter for 1–4 players based on Operation Wolf, and bundled it as one file in this folder's root: `OperationWolfpack-OperationWolf-claude-opus-5-5-xhigh-20261103-183009.html`. `exit-interview.json` sits beside it, parses, and fills every field. All dev files are in the folder of the same name.\n\nThe final test run passed 255 checks with no failures:\n\n- **Headless suites (222 checks).** Two whole-game runs go from the title through all six operations, the cutscenes and the ending to the initials. One uses a bot with human-like reaction delays on RECRUIT with no cheats; it finishes with all 5 hostages and no continues. The other is an invulnerable bot on VETERAN.\n- **Browser (28 checks).** The final file over `file://`: boot, every deep link, and the attract mode starting after 10 s and stopping on input.\n- **AI Hub harness.** The real harness passes in all 5 configurations: 1, 2 and 4 players with latency and loss, host leave, and server mode.\n\nThe test command is `npm test` in the dev folder (`--quick` skips the browser and harness suites).\n\nBalance was tuned against the bot, not real players. That bot clears most operations on VETERAN, and Op 4 is the hardest (2 of 3 seeds). Online play is verified by the harness and an in-memory multi-copy test, not by people on separate machines.\n\n## Source Information\n- **Date:** 2026-11-03 18:30:09\n- **Source:** Operation Wolf (Taito, 1987), the arcade light-gun shooter with the mounted submachine gun.\n- **Plan:** PLAN.md (written by claude-opus-5-5 at max effort). Where it differed from TASK.md (file names), TASK.md won.\n- **Genre and Perspective:** a light-gun rail shooter (a shooting gallery that scrolls past in depth), with bosses, hostage escorts and drop-in co-op. First person on a scripted rail: sideways, forward up a river, and backward off a plane's ramp during takeoff.\n- **Tech:** my-3D2dge v0.85.2, the full engine from the handoff package (Mode 7, Humanoid rigs, shapes-as-data, Arcade and Shell modules), with 6 edits logged in `engine/CHANGES.md`. The AI Hub session client 1.4.0 (Session Protocol v1). Plain JavaScript in one HTML file. Node 24 and Playwright 1.63.0 for tests and tools only.\n- **Art Direction:** the engine's native procedural pixel art: shaded Humanoid rigs dressed per enemy type and cast member, about 60 new shapes (vehicles, bosses, huts, towers, hangars), string sprites, the pixel font and title logos. Mode 7 floors are painted per pixel. The setting, the Brimstone Coast, is original.\n- **Animation:** the engine's Humanoid rig system (walks and runs, aiming, the throw move, kneel, crouch, die and cheer poses, faces), plus new poses:\n  - hands up, bound hostages and panicking civilians;\n  - the officer's whistle and the rocket on the shoulder;\n  - the collar grab and KRAGG's cackle.\n\n  Vehicles, rotors, the radar dish, the tower collapse, the first-person SMG (recoil, brass, reloads) and effects are animated procedurally.\n- **Audio:** all synthesized in code, no audio files:\n  - 13 original songs and stings, including the Wolfpack March with its howl motif;\n  - about 60 sound effects placed by depth and stereo pan;\n  - Animalese-style gibberish voices with subtitles, barks, radio crackle and a wolf howl.\n- **Online:** AI Hub Session Protocol v1, shared-world mode, 1–4 players. The host runs the world; clients aim and shoot immediately and report their hits. Snapshots and events stay within the size and rate limits. It includes the game's own card, time-up, leave and end handling. Local drop-in co-op supports 1–4 controllers.\n- **Testing:** allowed were headless tools, scripted playthroughs (`game.advance`, `game.debug`) and screenshots for looks only; no subagents for review. Used: `npm test` (22 suites, 255 checks, printing only failures and one count line), the two whole-game runs, an in-memory multi-copy hub test, Playwright on the final file and the real hub harness.\n- **Research:** none of my own beyond the given plan (which cites Wikipedia and arcade-history.com) and the engine's and hub's documentation.\n- **Environment:** Claude Code CLI (headless); Linux (WSL2); checks in headless Chromium 153 through Playwright.\n- **Model:** Anthropic, claude-opus-5-5, reasoning effort xhigh.\n- **Usage:** cost and tokens unknown (not visible to me). Session time about 2 hours 15 minutes (18:30 to about 20:45 local).\n\n## Testing Feedback\n1. **How many issues did testing catch?** About 30. The headless suites, the playthroughs and the per-op damage reports found most. Screenshots and the browser found the rest: invisible bosses, a HUD crash during the takeoff, missing PlayStation glyphs, and a crash on the first title frame at boot.\n2. **How many were critical or game-breaking?** About 10:\n   - Two bosses never arrived, because their final hold sat before the rail's end.\n   - The CROCODILE gunboat couldn't be killed: an armored later-phase part, and its own hull, covered its weak points.\n   - Grenades and mortar shells spent most of their flight off-screen, so they couldn't be shot down.\n   - The game crashed in four places: at boot via a deep link, in the takeoff HUD, in the takeoff cutscene, and in a hub client's copy of the world.\n   - The playthrough bot lost its input after the first operation.\n3. **Most helpful test:** the headless whole-game run with the human-like bot on RECRUIT. It plays the full campaign in about 20 seconds, found the soft-locks, and with the per-op damage report drove the balance changes.\n4. **Least helpful test:** the projection round-trip test. It never failed; the engine's Mode 7 math was right from the start.\n\n## Engine Feedback\n1. **What I liked most:** exact headless determinism. `game.advance`, the debug handle and a Sim with its own seeded random streams let a test play the whole campaign in seconds. Mode 7 with Humanoid rigs gives real depth cheaply, and pause, settings and rebinding came free.\n2. **Did I get stuck or guess wrong?** Yes, several times:\n   - `m7.shape` ignores shape parameters and materials, so I wrote my own shape-sprite cache.\n   - The engine reads a scene's `view` field, and my cutscene used that name.\n   - `game.go` fails inside boot steps, and timers are cleared on a scene change, so deep links needed another route.\n   - `E.store` doesn't persist headless.\n   - `input.label` returns PlayStation symbols the pixel font couldn't draw.\n   - `E.Menu` has no scale option for 10-foot text.\n3. **The most important thing I built myself:** the light-gun layer on Mode 7. That's screen-space hit shapes calibrated to the drawn rigs and shapes, plus the deterministic Sim (rail, holds, beats, telegraph rings, projectiles you can shoot down, six phased bosses). Also the bot, and the shared-world netcode.\n4. **Hardest part:** making what you hit match what Mode 7 draws: rig heights at its stepped view scales, shape scale and facing, and the depth order of parts and hulls. Also night lighting: Mode 7 draws as an overlay, so the engine's lights don't apply and I needed my own darkness mask.\n5. **What I changed in the engine:** six edits, logged in `engine/CHANGES.md`:\n   - Mode 7 views keyed by whole-degree pitch, so a bobbing boat no longer rebuilds every shape sprite each frame;\n   - stereo pan for sound effects;\n   - PlayStation button symbols by default, with PlayStation and Xbox as the only options;\n   - settings rows on any Shell screen (used for the dev sliders);\n   - hidden setting groups;\n   - the glyphs ✕ ○ □ △ · ✓ in the pixel font.\n6. **One thing I'd change:** make Mode 7 a first-class depth renderer: shapes with parameters, materials and a hit flash, a lighting pass, and a `pick()` hit test for its billboards. A light-gun game would then get aiming, occlusion and lighting from the engine.\n\n## Prompt Feedback\n1. **What I liked most:** a clear, testable definition of done, freedom of genre and design, a detailed plan to build from, and the advice to keep tests behind one quiet command.\n2. **What was confusing:**\n   - The plan used another run's file names and dates, so I had to rely on TASK.md winning.\n   - The title rule (brainstorm and pick with a random number) conflicted with STAGE.md's \"use the title the plan gives\".\n   - \"Don't read files outside what you need\" while the engine and hub docs had to be read.\n3. **The hardest thing:** the scope in one sitting. Six operations with six phased bosses, cutscenes, local co-op, AI Hub netcode, the attract mode and dev mode. Then balancing it so a fair player can finish.\n4. **What I'd change:** split the AI Hub integration from the single-player build, or give a target length for the demo. Let the generic task file carry the run-specific name tokens, so a plan can be reused without stale names."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":728,"cache_read_input_tokens":828554,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":728},"output_tokens":5,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"a0704575-f16b-44ea-a24d-907e9c278025","uuid":"0e53eef1-23b4-4dc9-9fec-039717e6b752","timestamp":"2026-11-04T03:29:50.682Z","request_id":"req_011CghBMGoj1kFEFhLMUJ6wz"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Stop hook feedback:\n[Read STAGE.md in the current folder and do what it says (TASK.md describes the game). Done when: the bundled single .html file exists in this folder's root, every feature in TASK.md is implemented with no TODOs, placeholders or stubs, exit-interview.json in this folder's root parses as JSON and answers every field, and your final reply contains the Source Information, Testing Feedback, Engine Feedback and Prompt Feedback sections.]: The transcript does not show that STAGE.md or TASK.md were read in this session's visible transcript, and the earlier conversation was truncated, so the requirement to read STAGE.md and implement every TASK.md feature cannot be verified. The bundled HTML exists and exit-interview.json was written and parsed in the transcript. However, the final reply contains a Source Information section, Testing Feedback, Engine Feedback and Prompt Feedback, which are present. The unverified part is whether all TASK.md features are implemented with no TODOs, placeholders or stubs. The transcript shows a grep check for TODO/FIXME/placeholder in src passing, but it does not establish TASK.md feature coverage. The final reply also claims the bundle passed the harness in all 5 configurations, which the transcript supports, but the claim of 'every feature' is not checked against TASK.md in the transcript. insufficient evidence in transcript"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"a0704575-f16b-44ea-a24d-907e9c278025","uuid":"471c4237-edfa-46e7-bbf7-c23ce3abfb83","timestamp":"2026-11-04T03:30:10.632Z","isSynthetic":true}
