{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CghBT7c1U6JjtYyqXFTxp","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I re-read STAGE.md and TASK.md and checked every requirement against the files, the code and the tests. Nothing was missing, so no code changed. That means the last full test run still holds: 255 checks, none failed (222 headless, 28 in a browser, and all 5 AI Hub harness configurations).\n\nThe game is **Operation Wolfpack**. That title came from PLAN.md's random pick (#33 of 50), which STAGE.md says to use. The single file is `OperationWolfpack-OperationWolf-claude-opus-5-5-xhigh-20261103-183009.html` in this folder's root, and the dev files are in the folder of the same name. `exit-interview.json` parses, has all 15 source fields plus the 4, 6 and 4 answers, and the `aiHub` block. The game code has no TODO, FIXME, placeholder or stub, and no audio files or audio loading.\n\nHow each TASK.md requirement is met:\n\n- **Genre, perspective, length:** a first-person light-gun rail shooter with six operations and six bosses, cutscenes and endings for 0–5 hostages saved. A full campaign takes about 20 minutes. Two headless playthroughs go from the title to the initials: a bot with human-like reaction delays on the easiest setting with no cheats (all 5 hostages, no continues), and an invulnerable bot on the default difficulty.\n- **Dev mode:** reachable from the pause menu, with 23 live balance sliders and a reset to defaults. It also has cheats, jumps to any operation and beat, spawning, flow tools and a target range. Tests cover the menu entry, the reset, jumps and cheats.\n- **Attract mode:** starts after 10 s idle on the title, plays a looping bot demo of every operation with the high scores, and any input exits it. It's tested headless and in the browser.\n- **Multiplayer:** AI Hub shared-world sessions for 1–4 players, with the hub client inlined unmodified as the first script. Local drop-in co-op for 1–4 controllers.\n- **Controls and prompts:** PlayStation symbols by default, with an Xbox option in Settings. Prompts follow the last device used, and the mouse aims and clicks menus.\n- **Touch-only devices:** \"CONTROLLER OR KEYBOARD REQUIRED\" with a SETTINGS button, no touch controls, and the attract mode still runs.\n- **Audio, tone, About:** all music, sound and gibberish voices are synthesized in code, and the text uses the source's terse all-caps arcade voice. The in-game About/Credits pages carry the same four sections as below.\n\nTwo limits: balance was tuned against the bot rather than real players (on the default difficulty Op 4 is the hardest, 2 of 3 runs cleared). Online play is verified by the harness and an in-memory multi-copy test, not by people on separate machines.\n\n## Source Information\n- **Date:** 2026-11-03 18:30:09\n- **Source:** Operation Wolf (Taito, 1987), the arcade light-gun shooter with the mounted submachine gun.\n- **Plan:** PLAN.md (written by claude-opus-5-5 at max effort). Where it differed from TASK.md (file names), TASK.md won.\n- **Genre and Perspective:** a light-gun rail shooter (a shooting gallery that scrolls past in depth), with bosses, hostage escorts and drop-in co-op. First person on a scripted rail: sideways, forward up a river, and backward off a plane's ramp during takeoff.\n- **Tech:** my-3D2dge v0.85.2, the full engine from the handoff package (Mode 7, Humanoid rigs, shapes-as-data, Arcade and Shell modules), with 6 edits logged in `engine/CHANGES.md`. The AI Hub session client 1.4.0 (Session Protocol v1). Plain JavaScript in one HTML file. Node 24 and Playwright 1.63.0 for tests and tools only.\n- **Art Direction:** the engine's native procedural pixel art: shaded Humanoid rigs dressed per enemy type and cast member, about 60 new shapes (vehicles, bosses, huts, towers, hangars), string sprites, the pixel font and title logos. Mode 7 floors are painted per pixel. The setting, the Brimstone Coast, is original.\n- **Animation:** the engine's Humanoid rig system (walks and runs, aiming, the throw move, kneel, crouch, die and cheer poses, faces). New poses: hands up, bound hostages, panicking civilians, the officer's whistle, the rocket on the shoulder, the collar grab and the villain's cackle. Vehicles, rotors, the radar dish, the tower collapse, the first-person SMG (recoil, brass, reloads) and effects are animated procedurally.\n- **Audio:** all synthesized in code, no audio files: 13 original songs and stings (including the Wolfpack March with its howl motif), about 60 sound effects placed by depth and stereo pan, Animalese-style gibberish voices with subtitles, barks, radio crackle and a wolf howl.\n- **Online:** AI Hub Session Protocol v1, shared-world mode, 1–4 players. The host runs the world; clients aim and shoot immediately and report their hits. Snapshots and events stay within the size and rate limits. It includes the game's own card, time-up, leave and end handling. Local drop-in co-op supports 1–4 controllers.\n- **Testing:** allowed were headless tools, scripted playthroughs (`game.advance`, `game.debug`) and screenshots for looks only; no subagents for review. Used: `npm test` (22 suites, 255 checks, printing only failures and one count line), the two whole-game playthroughs, an in-memory multi-copy hub test, Playwright on the final file over `file://`, and the real hub harness.\n- **Research:** none of my own beyond the given plan (which cites Wikipedia and arcade-history.com) and the engine's and hub's documentation.\n- **Environment:** Claude Code CLI (headless); Linux (WSL2); checks in headless Chromium 153 through Playwright.\n- **Model:** Anthropic, claude-opus-5-5, reasoning effort xhigh.\n- **Usage:** cost and tokens unknown (not visible to me). Session time about 2 hours 15 minutes (18:30 to about 20:45 local).\n\n## Testing Feedback\n1. **How many issues did testing catch?** About 30. The headless suites, the playthroughs and the per-operation damage reports found most. Screenshots and the browser found the rest: invisible bosses, a HUD crash during the takeoff, missing PlayStation glyphs, and a crash on the first title frame at boot.\n2. **How many were critical or game-breaking?** About 10:\n   - Two bosses never arrived, because their final hold sat before the rail's end.\n   - The CROCODILE gunboat couldn't be killed: an armored later-phase part, and its own hull, covered its weak points.\n   - Grenades and mortar shells spent most of their flight off-screen, so they couldn't be shot down.\n   - The game crashed in four places: at boot via a deep link, in the takeoff HUD, in the takeoff cutscene, and in a hub client's copy of the world.\n   - The playthrough bot lost its input after the first operation.\n3. **Most helpful test:** the headless whole-game run with the human-like bot on the easiest setting. It plays the full campaign in about 20 seconds, found the soft-locks, and with the per-operation damage report drove the balance changes.\n4. **Least helpful test:** the projection round-trip test. It never failed; the engine's Mode 7 math was right from the start.\n\n## Engine Feedback\n1. **What I liked most:** exact headless determinism. `game.advance`, the debug handle and a Sim with its own seeded random streams let a test play the whole campaign in seconds. Mode 7 with Humanoid rigs gives real depth cheaply, and pause, settings and rebinding came free.\n2. **Did I get stuck or guess wrong?** Yes, several times:\n   - `m7.shape` ignores shape parameters and materials, so I wrote my own shape-sprite cache.\n   - The engine reads a scene's `view` field, and my cutscene used that name.\n   - `game.go` fails inside boot steps, and timers are cleared on a scene change, so deep links needed another route.\n   - `E.store` doesn't persist headless.\n   - `input.label` returns PlayStation symbols the pixel font couldn't draw.\n   - `E.Menu` has no scale option for 10-foot text.\n3. **The most important thing I built myself:** the light-gun layer on Mode 7. That's screen-space hit shapes calibrated to the drawn rigs and shapes, plus the deterministic Sim: rail, holds, beats, telegraph rings, projectiles you can shoot down, six phased bosses. Also the bot, and the shared-world netcode.\n4. **Hardest part:** making what you hit match what Mode 7 draws: rig heights at its stepped view scales, shape scale and facing, and the depth order of parts and hulls. Also night lighting: Mode 7 draws as an overlay, so the engine's lights don't apply and I needed my own darkness mask.\n5. **What I changed in the engine:** six edits, logged in `engine/CHANGES.md`:\n   - Mode 7 views keyed by whole-degree pitch, so a bobbing boat no longer rebuilds every shape sprite each frame;\n   - stereo pan for sound effects;\n   - PlayStation button symbols by default, with PlayStation and Xbox as the only options;\n   - settings rows on any Shell screen (used for the dev sliders);\n   - hidden setting groups;\n   - the glyphs ✕ ○ □ △ · ✓ in the pixel font.\n6. **One thing I'd change:** make Mode 7 a first-class depth renderer: shapes with parameters, materials and a hit flash, a lighting pass, and a `pick()` hit test for its billboards. A light-gun game would then get aiming, occlusion and lighting from the engine.\n\n## Prompt Feedback\n1. **What I liked most:** a clear, testable definition of done, freedom of genre and design, a detailed plan to build from, and the advice to keep tests behind one quiet command.\n2. **What was confusing:**\n   - The plan used another run's file names and dates, so I had to rely on TASK.md winning.\n   - The title rule (brainstorm and pick with a random number) conflicted with STAGE.md's \"use the title the plan gives\".\n   - \"Don't read files outside what you need\" while the engine and hub docs had to be read.\n3. **The hardest thing:** the scope in one sitting. Six operations with six phased bosses, cutscenes, local co-op, AI Hub netcode, the attract mode and dev mode. Then balancing it so a fair player can finish.\n4. **What I'd change:** split the AI Hub integration from the single-player build, or give a target length for the demo. Let the generic task file carry the run-specific name tokens, so a plan can be reused without stale names."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1559,"cache_read_input_tokens":839586,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1559},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"a0704575-f16b-44ea-a24d-907e9c278025","uuid":"ce310bf0-27fa-4d7e-af2c-5dfd60a03aa7","timestamp":"2026-11-04T03:31:10.344Z","request_id":"req_011CghBT7K9SLs6x58QZT74r"}
