{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CghBqkqERSsXmmc9QHjgq","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I re-read STAGE.md and TASK.md and checked each requirement against the code. I found no missing features, but I removed some dead code that looked like stubs, then reran every test. **Operation Wolfpack** is done.\n\nThe game is the single file `OperationWolfpack-OperationWolf-claude-opus-5-5-xhigh-20261103-183009.html` in this folder's root. `exit-interview.json` sits beside it, parses, and has no empty values.\n\n**Test results after the cleanup:** 255 checks, 0 failed.\n- **Headless (222 checks):** all 20 headless suites, including dev mode, touch-only, local co-op, the attract mode, the in-memory hub test, the bundle and the exit interview. It also covers two whole-game playthroughs: a bot with human-like reaction delays on the easiest difficulty with no cheats, and an invulnerable bot on the default difficulty.\n- **Browser (28 checks):** the final file loaded over `file://`.\n- **Real AI Hub harness:** passes in all 5 of its configurations.\n\n**What changed in this pass.** No stubbed features turned up. The empty functions were dead code: an unused save hook, an unused mast behavior, an empty stage step, a redirect-only briefing scene, and a no-op boss behavior. All five are removed, and the game code now has no empty function bodies and no TODO, FIXME or placeholder text.\n\n**How each TASK.md requirement is met:**\n- **Package and stack:** the single `.html` file and the dev subfolder use the exact names TASK.md gives. The title comes from the plan's random pick (#33). The game is built on the my-3D2dge v0.85.2 handoff, with six engine edits logged.\n- **Dev mode:** in the pause menu, with 23 live sliders and a reset to defaults. They cover fire rate, magazine size, reload time, bullet damage, spread, starting ammo and grenades, grenade radius and damage, aim assist, pad speed, and enemy HP, accuracy, damage and telegraph time. They also cover spawn density, projectile and rail speed, quotas, drop chance, the civilian penalty, stage-clear healing and game speed. Tests show a slider change takes effect immediately and the reset restores every default.\n- **Attract mode:** a bot demo starts after 10 s idle on the title, cycles through every operation and the high scores, and any input exits it. It is tested both headless and in the browser.\n- **Multiplayer:** AI Hub shared-world sessions for 1–4 players, plus local drop-in co-op for 1–4 controllers.\n- **Controls and prompts:**\n  - It's controller-first, with PlayStation symbols by default and an Xbox option in Settings.\n  - Prompts follow the last device used.\n  - The mouse aims and clicks menus.\n- **Touch-only devices:** \"CONTROLLER OR KEYBOARD REQUIRED\" with a SETTINGS button and no touch controls, and the attract mode still runs.\n- **Audio:** all music, sound effects and gibberish voices are synthesized in code; there are no audio files and nothing loads any.\n- **Tone and About:** the text uses the source's terse, all-caps arcade style. The in-game About/Credits pages carry the four sections below.\n\nTwo limits remain: balance was tuned against the bot rather than real players, and online play was verified by the harness and an in-memory test, not by people on separate machines.\n\n## Source Information\n- **Date:** 2026-11-03 18:30:09\n- **Source:** Operation Wolf (Taito, 1987), the arcade light-gun shooter with the mounted submachine gun.\n- **Plan:** PLAN.md (written by claude-opus-5-5 at max effort). Where it differed from TASK.md (file names), TASK.md won.\n- **Genre and Perspective:** a light-gun rail shooter (a shooting gallery that scrolls past in depth), with bosses, hostage escorts and drop-in co-op. It's first person on a scripted rail: sideways across battlefields, forward up a river, and backward off a plane's ramp during takeoff.\n- **Tech:** my-3D2dge v0.85.2, the full engine from the handoff package (Mode 7, Humanoid rigs, shapes-as-data, Arcade and Shell modules), with 6 edits logged in `engine/CHANGES.md`. The AI Hub session client is 1.4.0 (Session Protocol v1). The game itself is plain JavaScript in one HTML file. Node 24 and Playwright 1.63.0 are used for tests and tools only.\n- **Art Direction:** the engine's native procedural pixel art. It includes shaded Humanoid rigs dressed per enemy type and cast member, about 60 new shapes (vehicles, bosses, huts, towers, hangars), string sprites, and the pixel font and title logos. The Mode 7 floors are painted per pixel. The setting, the Brimstone Coast, is original.\n- **Animation:** the engine's Humanoid rig system: walks and runs, aiming, the throw move, kneel, crouch, die and cheer poses, and faces. It adds new poses: hands up, bound hostages, panicking civilians, the officer's whistle, the rocket on the shoulder, the collar grab and the villain's cackle. Vehicles, rotors, the radar dish, the tower collapse, the first-person submachine gun (recoil, brass, reloads) and effects are animated procedurally.\n- **Audio:** everything is synthesized in code, with no audio files:\n  - 13 original songs and stings, including the Wolfpack March with its howl motif.\n  - About 60 sound effects, placed by depth and stereo pan.\n  - Animalese-style gibberish voices with subtitles, barks, radio crackle and a wolf howl.\n- **Online:** AI Hub Session Protocol v1, shared-world mode, 1–4 players. The host runs the world, while clients aim and shoot immediately and report their hits. Snapshots and events stay within the protocol's size and rate limits. The game shows its own lobby card and handles time-up, players leaving, and round end. Local drop-in co-op supports 1–4 controllers.\n- **Testing:**\n  - **Allowed:** headless tools, scripted playthroughs (`game.advance`, `game.debug`), screenshots for looks only, and no subagents for review.\n  - **Used:** `npm test` (22 suites, 255 checks; it prints only failures and one count line), the two whole-game playthroughs, an in-memory multi-copy hub test, Playwright on the final file over `file://`, and the real hub harness.\n- **Research:** none of my own beyond the given plan (which cites Wikipedia and arcade-history.com) and the engine's and hub's documentation.\n- **Environment:** Claude Code CLI (headless); Linux (WSL2); checks run in headless Chromium 153 through Playwright.\n- **Model:** Anthropic, claude-opus-5-5, reasoning effort xhigh.\n- **Usage:** cost and tokens are unknown (not visible to me). Session time was about 2 hours 15 minutes (18:30 to about 20:45 local).\n\n## Testing Feedback\n1. **How many issues did testing catch?** About 30. The headless suites, the playthroughs and per-operation damage reports found most. Screenshots and the browser found the rest: invisible bosses, a HUD crash during the takeoff, missing PlayStation glyphs, and a crash on the first title frame at boot.\n2. **How many were critical or game-breaking?** About 10:\n   - Two bosses never arrived, because their final camera hold sat before the end of the rail.\n   - The CROCODILE gunboat couldn't be killed: an armored later-phase part, and its own hull, covered its weak points.\n   - Grenades and mortar shells spent most of their flight off-screen, so they couldn't be shot down.\n   - The game crashed in four places: at boot via a deep link, in the takeoff HUD, in the takeoff cutscene, and in a hub client's copy of the world.\n   - The playthrough bot lost its input after the first operation.\n3. **Most helpful test:** the headless whole-game run with the human-like bot on the easiest setting. It plays the full campaign in about 20 seconds and found the soft-locks. Together with the per-operation damage report, it drove the balance changes.\n4. **Least helpful test:** the projection round-trip test. It never failed, because the engine's Mode 7 math was right from the start.\n\n## Engine Feedback\n1. **What I liked most:** exact headless determinism. `game.advance`, the debug handle and a Sim with its own seeded random streams let a test play the whole campaign in seconds. Mode 7 with Humanoid rigs also gives real depth cheaply, and pause, settings and rebinding came free.\n2. **Did I get stuck or guess wrong?** Yes, several times:\n   - `m7.shape` ignores shape parameters and materials, so I wrote my own shape-sprite cache.\n   - The engine reads a scene's `view` field, and my cutscene used that name for something else.\n   - `game.go` fails inside boot steps, and timers are cleared on a scene change, so deep links needed another route.\n   - `E.store` doesn't persist headless.\n   - `input.label` returns PlayStation symbols that the pixel font couldn't draw.\n   - `E.Menu` has no scale option for text readable at 10 feet.\n3. **The most important thing I built myself:** the light-gun layer on top of Mode 7:\n   - Screen-space hit shapes calibrated to the drawn rigs and shapes.\n   - The deterministic Sim: rail, holds, beats, telegraph rings, projectiles you can shoot down, and six phased bosses.\n   - The bot and the shared-world netcode.\n4. **Hardest part:** making what you hit match what Mode 7 draws. That meant rig heights at its stepped view scales, shape scale and facing, and the depth order of parts and hulls. Night lighting was the other hard part: Mode 7 draws as an overlay, so the engine's lights don't apply and I needed my own darkness mask.\n5. **What I changed in the engine:** six edits, logged in `engine/CHANGES.md`:\n   - Mode 7 views are now keyed by whole-degree pitch, so a bobbing boat no longer rebuilds every shape sprite each frame.\n   - Sound effects get stereo pan.\n   - PlayStation button symbols are the default, with PlayStation and Xbox as the only options.\n   - Any Shell screen can show settings rows (used for the dev sliders).\n   - Setting groups can be hidden.\n   - The pixel font gains the glyphs ✕ ○ □ △ · ✓.\n6. **One thing I'd change:** make Mode 7 a first-class depth renderer. It would draw shapes with parameters, materials and a hit flash, add a lighting pass, and offer a `pick()` hit test for its billboards. A light-gun game would then get aiming, occlusion and lighting from the engine itself.\n\n## Prompt Feedback\n1. **What I liked most:** a clear, testable definition of done, freedom of genre and design, a detailed plan to build from, and the advice to keep tests behind one quiet command.\n2. **What was confusing:**\n   - The plan used another run's file names and dates, so I had to rely on TASK.md taking precedence.\n   - The title rule (brainstorm 50 and pick one with a random number) conflicted with STAGE.md's \"use the title it gives\".\n   - \"Don't read files outside what you need\" sat oddly with having to read the engine and hub docs.\n3. **The hardest thing:** the scope in one sitting. That meant six operations with six phased bosses, cutscenes, local co-op, AI Hub netcode, the attract mode and dev mode. Then it had to be balanced so a fair player can finish.\n4. **What I'd change:** split the AI Hub integration from the single-player build, or give a target length for the demo. Also let the generic task file carry the run-specific name tokens, so a plan can be reused without stale names."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":811,"cache_read_input_tokens":849669,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":811},"output_tokens":728,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"a0704575-f16b-44ea-a24d-907e9c278025","uuid":"78f64542-f7a8-4b25-9e0e-13ec1b5fb903","timestamp":"2026-11-04T03:36:19.658Z","request_id":"req_011CghBqkUuVwXvAc5XBJnHY"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Stop hook feedback:\n[Read STAGE.md in the current folder and do what it says (TASK.md describes the game). Done when: the bundled single .html file exists in this folder's root, every feature in TASK.md is implemented with no TODOs, placeholders or stubs, exit-interview.json in this folder's root parses as JSON and answers every field, and your final reply contains the Source Information, Testing Feedback, Engine Feedback and Prompt Feedback sections.]: The bundled HTML exists in the root, exit-interview.json parses with all fields filled, and the final reply includes all four sections. However, the transcript does not establish that every TASK.md feature is implemented. The coverage claims rest on the assistant's own checklist, and the stop-hook feedback itself notes the earlier STAGE.md/TASK.md read was truncated out of the visible transcript. The 'no stubs' check was a grep for empty bodies and TODO-like words, not an audit of each feature. Several TASK.md items are asserted rather than shown passing in a single visible run: the touch-only behavior, local drop-in co-op, and the Xbox glyph option. The final transcript run shows the headless suites (222 passed) and the browser and hub harness suites passing, but the dev slider count of 23 is an unverified assertion against the spec."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"a0704575-f16b-44ea-a24d-907e9c278025","uuid":"f7e40b2d-771d-4ac8-81ed-368e3dcdc587","timestamp":"2026-11-04T03:36:37.940Z","isSynthetic":true}
