{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CghCMZ59jC1RwWsoGSa5K","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The game is finished and every TASK.md requirement is shown passing in this session. I re-read STAGE.md and TASK.md, then wrote `tests/audit.mjs` (`npm run audit`), which exercises each requirement and prints PASS or FAIL with evidence. Its last run was 34 passed, 0 failed.\n\nThe audit's first run had one real failure: menus ignored mouse hover in the headless test. I fixed it, and since that changed the menu code, I reran the whole suite: 222 headless checks, 28 browser checks on the final file, and all 5 real AI Hub harness configurations, with no failures.\n\nWhat the audit run shows:\n\n- **Package:** `OperationWolfpack-OperationWolf-claude-opus-5-5-xhigh-20261103-183009.html` is in the root with no external scripts, and the dev files are in the matching folder. The title comes from the plan's random pick (#33), and the game is built on the my-3d2dge v0.85.2 handoff.\n- **Scope:** 6 operations, 6 bosses, 11 pickups, 8 projectile kinds and 10 infantry types, plus vehicles, boats, aircraft, civilians, hostages and supply animals.\n- **Art and animation:** 66 new shapes, 35 sprites and 23 rig presets, plus new poses on the engine's rig system.\n- **Audio:** 17 songs and 58 sound effects, all synthesized in code, with gibberish voices; there are no audio files.\n- **Dev mode:** reachable from the pause menu. All 23 sliders were each set and change the game immediately:\n  - weapon: fire rate, magazine size, reload time, damage, spread;\n  - loadout: starting magazines and grenades, grenade radius and damage;\n  - aiming: aim assist, pad speed;\n  - enemies: HP, accuracy, damage, telegraph time, spawn density;\n  - pacing: projectile speed, rail speed, quotas, drop chance, civilian penalty, stage-clear heal, game speed.\n\n  Reset to defaults restores all of them, and the standard tools are there.\n- **Attract mode:** still on the title at 9.5 s, a cheating bot demo at 11.1 s, and a button press returns to the title.\n- **Multiplayer:** a second controller drops in mid-operation, with play for up to 4. AI Hub shared-world mode passes both a 3-copy simulated session and the real harness.\n- **UI:**\n  - PlayStation symbols by default (R2 □ ✕), with an Xbox option in Settings (RT X A).\n  - Keyboard and mouse get their own prompts, which follow the last device used.\n  - The mouse aims absolutely, and hovering or clicking works in menus.\n  - Screens use 10-foot sizes.\n- **Touch-only devices:** no touch controls, the \"CONTROLLER OR KEYBOARD REQUIRED\" notice with a working SETTINGS button, and the attract mode still runs.\n- **Done:** no TODO, placeholder or stub text and no empty function bodies in the game code. The About screen has all four sections plus credits, and `exit-interview.json` parses with every field filled.\n\nTwo limits: balance was tuned against the bot rather than real players, and online play was verified by the harness and the simulated session, not by people on separate machines.\n\n## Source Information\n- **Date:** 2026-11-03 18:30:09\n- **Source:** Operation Wolf (Taito, 1987), the arcade light-gun shooter with the mounted submachine gun.\n- **Plan:** PLAN.md (written by claude-opus-5-5 at max effort). Where it differed from TASK.md (file names), TASK.md won.\n- **Genre and Perspective:** a light-gun rail shooter (a shooting gallery that scrolls past in depth), with bosses, hostage escorts and drop-in co-op. It's first person on a scripted rail: sideways across the battlefield, forward up a river, and backward off a plane's ramp during takeoff.\n- **Tech:** my-3D2dge v0.85.2, the full engine from the handoff package, with 6 edits logged in `engine/CHANGES.md`. The AI Hub session client is 1.4.0 (Session Protocol v1). The game is plain JavaScript in one HTML file. Node 24 and Playwright 1.63.0 are used for tests and tools only.\n- **Art Direction:** the engine's native procedural pixel art, with about 60 new shapes, string sprites, the pixel font and title logos. The Mode 7 floors are painted per pixel. The setting, the Brimstone Coast, is original.\n- **Animation:** the engine's Humanoid rig system, plus new poses: hands up, bound hostages, panic, the officer's whistle, the rocket on the shoulder, the collar grab and the villain's cackle. Vehicles, the first-person gun and the effects are animated procedurally.\n- **Audio:** everything is synthesized in code with no audio files: original songs and stings, about 60 sound effects placed by depth and stereo pan, Animalese-style gibberish voices with subtitles, barks and a wolf howl.\n- **Online:** AI Hub Session Protocol v1 in shared-world mode for 1–4 players, with the host running the world and clients reporting hits. The game shows its own lobby card and handles time-up, players leaving, and the round ending. Local drop-in co-op supports 1–4 controllers.\n- **Testing:** allowed were headless tools, scripted playthroughs and screenshots for looks only, with no subagents. I used `npm test` (22 suites, 255 checks, printing only failures and one count line), which includes two whole-game playthroughs, a simulated multi-copy hub test, browser checks on the final file and the real harness. `npm run audit` is the per-requirement report.\n- **Research:** none of my own beyond the given plan (which cites Wikipedia and arcade-history.com) and the engine's and hub's documentation.\n- **Environment:** Claude Code CLI (headless); Linux (WSL2); checks ran in headless Chromium 153 through Playwright.\n- **Model:** Anthropic, claude-opus-5-5, reasoning effort xhigh.\n- **Usage:** cost and tokens unknown (not visible to me). Session time was about 2 hours 15 minutes, which I estimated while writing the About pages; the final checks ran a little past that.\n\n## Testing Feedback\n1. **How many issues did testing catch?** About 30. The headless suites, the playthroughs and per-operation damage reports caught most of them. Screenshots and the browser caught the rest: bosses that were never drawn, HUD and cutscene crashes, glyphs missing from the font, and a crash on the first title frame at boot.\n2. **How many were critical or game-breaking?** About 10:\n   - Two bosses never arrived.\n   - The CROCODILE gunboat couldn't be killed.\n   - Grenades and mortar shells couldn't be shot down.\n   - The game crashed at boot, in the takeoff, and in a hub client's copy of the world.\n   - The playthrough bot lost its input after the first operation.\n3. **Most helpful test:** the headless whole-game playthrough with a bot that has human-like reaction delays, on the easiest difficulty with no cheats. It found the soft-locks and drove the balance changes.\n4. **Least helpful test:** the projection round-trip test. It never failed.\n\n## Engine Feedback\n1. **What I liked most:** exact headless determinism (`game.advance`, the debug handle, seeded random streams), so the whole campaign tests in seconds. Mode 7 with Humanoid rigs gives real depth cheaply, and pause, settings and rebinding came free.\n2. **Did I get stuck or guess wrong?** Yes:\n   - `m7.shape` ignores shape parameters and materials.\n   - The engine reads a scene's `view` field, which my cutscene used for something else.\n   - `game.go` fails inside boot steps, and timers are cleared on scene change, so deep links needed another route.\n   - `E.store` doesn't persist headless.\n   - The font lacked the PlayStation symbols that `input.label` returns.\n   - `E.Menu` has no scale for text readable at 10 feet.\n3. **The most important thing I built myself:** the light-gun layer on Mode 7: hit shapes calibrated to the drawn rigs, the deterministic Sim (rail, beats, telegraph rings, projectiles you can shoot down, six phased bosses), the bot, and the shared-world netcode.\n4. **Hardest part:** making what you hit match what Mode 7 draws, and night lighting under Mode 7, which needed its own darkness mask.\n5. **What I changed in the engine:** six edits, logged in `engine/CHANGES.md`:\n   - Mode 7 drawing views keyed by whole-degree pitch, so a bobbing camera reuses its cached sprites;\n   - stereo pan for sound effects;\n   - PlayStation glyphs by default, with an Xbox option;\n   - settings rows on any Shell screen;\n   - hidden setting groups;\n   - the glyphs ✕ ○ □ △ · ✓ in the pixel font.\n6. **One thing I'd change:** make Mode 7 a first-class depth renderer: shapes with parameters, materials and a hit flash, a lighting pass, and a `pick()` hit test for its billboards.\n\n## Prompt Feedback\n1. **What I liked most:** a clear, testable definition of done, freedom of design, a detailed plan, and one quiet test command.\n2. **What was confusing:** the plan carried another run's file names, and the title rule conflicted with STAGE.md's \"use the title it gives\". \"Don't read files outside what you need\" also sat oddly beside docs that had to be read.\n3. **The hardest thing:** the scope in one sitting (six operations, six bosses, co-op, the AI Hub netcode, attract mode, dev mode), and balancing it so a fair player can finish.\n4. **What I'd change:** split the AI Hub integration from the single-player build, or give a target demo length, and let the task file carry the run-specific name tokens."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":326,"cache_read_input_tokens":871366,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":326},"output_tokens":5,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"a0704575-f16b-44ea-a24d-907e9c278025","uuid":"b5058dd8-4631-43ec-83f6-2bb65373a199","timestamp":"2026-11-04T03:43:00.832Z","request_id":"req_011CghCMYmJ3ZRxxw67cgDao"}
