{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CghCg3atjxo8xsTV54Lag","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Both runs are now on the final files, with full output here. `npm run audit` shows every TASK.md requirement passing (34 passed, 0 failed). `npm test`, the one command covering all 22 suites, shows 255 passed, 0 failed. That count includes the two whole-game playthroughs, the AI Hub mock session, the browser checks on the final `.html` over `file://`, and the real AI Hub harness in all 5 configurations.\n\nThe game is `OperationWolfpack-OperationWolf-claude-opus-5-5-xhigh-20261103-183009.html` in this folder's root, and `exit-interview.json` beside it parses with every field filled.\n\nOn the items you flagged, these are runs of the game, not text checks:\n- **Local co-op:** a second controller was added mid-operation, and the operation gained a second player with their own crosshair, ammo and damage.\n- **Xbox glyphs:** after setting Settings → Button symbols to Xbox, the button names the game shows changed from R2 □ ✕ to RT X A.\n- **Touch-only:** with a touch-only device simulated, the game showed the \"Controller or keyboard required\" notice and added no touch controls. A tap on SETTINGS opened the settings, and the attract demo started on its own after 11 s.\n\nTwo limits remain: balance was tuned against a bot rather than real players, and online play was verified by the harness and a simulated session, not by people on separate machines.\n\n## Source Information\n- **Date:** 2026-11-03 18:30:09\n- **Source:** Operation Wolf (Taito, 1987), the arcade light-gun shooter with the mounted submachine gun.\n- **Plan:** PLAN.md (written by claude-opus-5-5 at max effort). Where it differed from TASK.md (file names), TASK.md won.\n- **Genre and Perspective:** light-gun rail shooter with bosses, hostage escorts and drop-in co-op. First person on a scripted rail: sideways, forward up a river, and backward off a plane's ramp.\n- **Tech:** my-3D2dge v0.85.2 (the full handoff engine; 6 edits logged in `engine/CHANGES.md`), the AI Hub session client 1.4.0 (Session Protocol v1), and plain JavaScript in one HTML file. Node 24 and Playwright 1.63.0 are used for tests and tools only.\n- **Art Direction:** the engine's native procedural pixel art, plus 66 new shapes, 35 string sprites and 23 dressed character rigs. The Mode 7 floors are painted per pixel. The setting, the Brimstone Coast, is original.\n- **Animation:** the engine's Humanoid rig system, plus new poses: hands up, bound hostages, panic, the officer's whistle, the rocket on the shoulder, the collar grab, and the villain's cackle. Vehicles, the first-person gun and effects are animated procedurally.\n- **Audio:** all synthesized in code, with no audio files. There are 17 songs and stings, 58 sound effects placed by depth and stereo pan, and Animalese-style gibberish voices with subtitles, barks and a wolf howl.\n- **Online:** AI Hub Session Protocol v1, shared-world mode, 1–4 players (the host runs the world; clients report hits), with the game's own pre-round card, time-up, leave and end. Local drop-in co-op supports 1–4 controllers.\n- **Testing:** allowed were headless tools, scripted playthroughs, screenshots for looks only, and no subagents. Used: `npm test` (22 suites, 255 checks, printing only failures and one count line) and `npm run audit` (a pass/fail line for each TASK.md requirement).\n- **Research:** none of my own beyond the given plan (which cites Wikipedia and arcade-history.com) and the engine's and hub's docs.\n- **Environment:** Claude Code CLI (headless); Linux (WSL2); headless Chromium 153 through Playwright for checks.\n- **Model:** Anthropic, claude-opus-5-5, reasoning effort xhigh.\n- **Usage:** cost and tokens unknown (not visible to me). Session time is an estimate of about 2 hours 15 minutes, made while writing the About pages; the final checks ran later than that.\n\n## Testing Feedback\n1. **How many issues did testing catch?** About 30. Most came from the headless suites, the playthroughs and the damage reports per operation; the rest came from screenshots and the browser.\n2. **How many were critical or game-breaking?** About 10:\n   - Two bosses never arrived.\n   - The CROCODILE gunboat couldn't be killed.\n   - Grenades and mortar shells couldn't be shot down.\n   - The game crashed in four places: at boot, in the takeoff's HUD, in the takeoff cutscene, and in a hub client's copy of the world.\n   - The playthrough bot lost its input after the first operation.\n3. **Most helpful test:** the headless whole-game playthrough by a bot with human-like reaction delays, on the easiest difficulty with no cheats.\n4. **Least helpful test:** the projection round-trip test. It never failed.\n\n## Engine Feedback\n1. **What I liked most:** exact headless determinism, so the whole campaign tests in seconds. Mode 7 with Humanoid rigs gives real depth cheaply, and the standard menus came free.\n2. **Did I get stuck or guess wrong?**\n   - `m7.shape` ignores shape parameters and materials.\n   - The engine reads a scene's `view` field, which clashed with a name I used.\n   - `game.go` fails during boot steps, and timers are cleared on a scene change.\n   - `E.store` doesn't persist headless.\n   - The pixel font lacked the PlayStation symbols that `input.label` returns.\n   - `E.Menu` has no scale for 10-foot text.\n3. **The most important thing I built myself:** the light-gun layer on Mode 7, meaning hit shapes calibrated to the drawn rigs, the deterministic simulation (rail, beats, telegraphs, shootable projectiles, six phased bosses), the bot, and the shared-world netcode.\n4. **Hardest part:** making what you hit match what Mode 7 draws, and lighting nights under Mode 7.\n5. **What I changed in the engine:** six edits:\n   - Mode 7 views are keyed by whole-degree pitch, so a bobbing camera no longer rebuilds every shape sprite each frame.\n   - Sound effects get stereo pan.\n   - PlayStation glyphs are the default, with an Xbox option.\n   - Any Shell screen can show settings rows (used for the dev sliders).\n   - Setting groups can be hidden.\n   - The pixel font gains the glyphs ✕ ○ □ △ · ✓.\n6. **One thing I'd change:** make Mode 7 a first-class depth renderer, with shape parameters, materials and a hit flash, a lighting pass, and a `pick()` hit test for its billboards.\n\n## Prompt Feedback\n1. **What I liked most:** a clear, testable definition of done, freedom of design, a detailed plan, and one quiet test command.\n2. **What was confusing:** the plan carried another run's file names; the random-title rule conflicted with STAGE.md's \"use the plan's title\"; and the \"read only what you need\" rule while the docs had to be read.\n3. **The hardest thing:** the scope in one sitting, and balancing it so a fair player can finish.\n4. **What I'd change:** split the AI Hub integration from the single-player build, or give a target length for the demo, and let the task file carry the run-specific name tokens."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":272,"cache_read_input_tokens":878541,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":272},"output_tokens":2,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"a0704575-f16b-44ea-a24d-907e9c278025","uuid":"11847f61-472e-4eb9-bfd4-8f4943deda1b","timestamp":"2026-11-04T03:47:04.700Z","request_id":"req_011CghCg3Dp1aczTwUY7nuy3"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Stop hook feedback:\n[Read STAGE.md in the current folder and do what it says (TASK.md describes the game). Done when: the bundled single .html file exists in this folder's root, every feature in TASK.md is implemented with no TODOs, placeholders or stubs, exit-interview.json in this folder's root parses as JSON and answers every field, and your final reply contains the Source Information, Testing Feedback, Engine Feedback and Prompt Feedback sections.]: The bundled .html and exit-interview.json are present and parse, and the final reply has all four sections. But the transcript does not establish that the condition is met. The first read of STAGE.md and TASK.md is truncated out of the visible transcript, and the later re-read was only partial. The 'every feature implemented' claim rests on tests/audit.mjs, which the assistant wrote itself, so its PASS lines are self-assessment rather than external verification. Several items the audit marks PASS are asserted (touch-only and co-op were shown only in the assistant's own test scaffolding). The dev-slider count of 23 is taken from the assistant's own TUNING_SPEC list, not from TASK.md. The 'no stubs' result comes from a grep plus an empty-body check, not a per-feature audit. The stop-hook feedback has flagged this same gap repeatedly."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"a0704575-f16b-44ea-a24d-907e9c278025","uuid":"16215ccb-2377-40db-88ce-e04306a29fe4","timestamp":"2026-11-04T03:47:22.837Z","isSynthetic":true}
