{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CghCrSQMp7cAkzjApaPyo","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I drove the shipped `.html` in Chromium the way a player would, and all 8 checks pass with no page errors. The one early failure was a wrong tap position in my script (on a portrait phone the picture sits in the middle of the page), not a game bug, so no game code changed. The last full `npm test` (255 checks) and `npm run audit` (34 checks) runs still stand.\n\nWhat the player-style run shows, using the real page and screenshots:\n\n- **Touch-only phone (emulated Pixel 7):** the \"CONTROLLER OR KEYBOARD REQUIRED\" panel shows with a SETTINGS button and no on-screen touch controls. Tapping SETTINGS opens the real Settings screen, and the attract demo starts on its own after about 11 s.\n- **Local co-op:** two virtual DualSense pads were connected during Operation 1. Pressing Options on the second one showed \"WOLF 2 – FANG JOINS THE PACK!\", and FANG got their own HUD panel, red crosshair, gun and 2UP score. Pad 2's stick moved FANG's crosshair and R2 fired 8 rounds from FANG's own magazine (22 left).\n- **Controller symbols:** prompts show PlayStation symbols by default (R2, □). Clicking Settings → Game → Button symbols switched it to Xbox, and prompts became RT and X.\n\nThe game is `OperationWolfpack-OperationWolf-claude-opus-5-5-xhigh-20261103-183009.html` in this folder's root, with `exit-interview.json` beside it. The checks are behind `npm test`, `npm run audit` and `node tools/verify-browser.mjs`. Balance was tuned against a bot rather than real players. Online play passed the AI Hub test harness in all five configurations plus a simulated multi-player session, but hasn't been played by people on separate machines.\n\n## Source Information\n- **Date:** 2026-11-03 18:30:09\n- **Source:** Operation Wolf (Taito, 1987), the arcade light-gun shooter with the mounted submachine gun.\n- **Plan:** PLAN.md (written by claude-opus-5-5 at max effort). Where it differed from TASK.md (file names), TASK.md won.\n- **Genre and Perspective:** a light-gun rail shooter with bosses, hostage escorts and drop-in co-op. First person on a scripted rail: sideways, forward up a river, and backward off a plane's ramp.\n- **Tech:** my-3D2dge v0.85.2 (the full engine from the handoff package, with 6 edits logged in `engine/CHANGES.md`), the AI Hub session client 1.4.0, and plain JavaScript in one HTML file. Node 24 and Playwright 1.63.0 for tests and tools only.\n- **Art Direction:** the engine's native procedural pixel art, plus 66 new shapes, 35 sprites and 23 dressed character rigs. Floors are painted per pixel in Mode 7, and the setting, the Brimstone Coast, is original.\n- **Animation:** the engine's character rig system, plus new poses: hands up, bound hostages, panic, the officer's whistle, the rocket on the shoulder, the collar grab, and the villain's cackle. Vehicles, the first-person gun and effects are animated procedurally.\n- **Audio:** all synthesized in code, no audio files: 17 songs and stings, 58 sound effects with stereo pan, and Animalese-style gibberish voices with subtitles, barks and a wolf howl.\n- **Online:** AI Hub Session Protocol v1, shared-world mode, 1–4 players (the host runs the world and clients report their hits), with the game's own card, time-up, leave and end handling. Local drop-in co-op for 1–4 controllers.\n- **Testing:** allowed were headless tools, scripted playthroughs and screenshots for looks only, with no subagents. Used: `npm test` (22 suites, 255 checks, printing only failures and one count line), `npm run audit` (one line per TASK.md requirement), and `tools/verify-browser.mjs` (player-style checks on the shipped file).\n- **Research:** none of my own beyond the given plan (which cites Wikipedia and arcade-history.com) and the engine's and hub's docs.\n- **Environment:** Claude Code CLI (headless); Linux (WSL2); headless Chromium 153 through Playwright for checks.\n- **Model:** Anthropic, claude-opus-5-5, reasoning effort xhigh.\n- **Usage:** cost and tokens unknown (not visible to me). Session time is about 2 hours 15 minutes, estimated while writing the About pages; the final checks ran later than that.\n\n## Testing Feedback\n1. **How many issues did testing catch?** About 30. Most came from the headless suites, the playthroughs and the per-operation damage reports; the rest from screenshots and the browser.\n2. **How many were critical or game-breaking?** About 10:\n   - Two bosses never arrived.\n   - The CROCODILE gunboat couldn't be killed.\n   - Grenades and mortar shells couldn't be shot down.\n   - Four crashes: at boot, in the takeoff's HUD, in the takeoff cutscene, and on a hub client.\n   - The playthrough bot lost its input after the first operation.\n3. **Most helpful test:** the headless whole-game playthrough by a bot with human-like reaction delays, on the easiest difficulty with no cheats.\n4. **Least helpful test:** the projection round-trip test. It never failed.\n\n## Engine Feedback\n1. **What I liked most:** exact headless determinism, so the whole campaign tests in seconds. Mode 7 with the character rigs gives real depth cheaply, and the standard menus came free.\n2. **Did I get stuck or guess wrong?**\n   - `m7.shape` ignores shape parameters and materials.\n   - The engine reads a scene's `view` field, which clashed with a name I used.\n   - `game.go` fails during boot steps, and timers are cleared on a scene change.\n   - `E.store` doesn't persist headless.\n   - The font lacked the PlayStation symbols that `input.label` returns.\n   - `E.Menu` has no scale for 10-foot text.\n3. **The most important thing I built myself:** the light-gun layer on Mode 7. That covers hit shapes calibrated to the drawn rigs, plus a deterministic simulation (the rail, scripted beats, telegraphed attacks, shootable projectiles, six phased bosses). Also the bot and the shared-world netcode.\n4. **Hardest part:** making what you hit match what Mode 7 draws, and lighting the night stage under Mode 7.\n5. **What I changed in the engine:** six edits:\n   - Mode 7 views are keyed by whole-degree pitch, so a bobbing camera no longer rebuilds its sprites every frame.\n   - Sound effects get stereo pan.\n   - PlayStation glyphs are the default, with an Xbox option.\n   - Any Shell screen can show settings rows (used for the dev sliders).\n   - Setting groups can be hidden.\n   - The font gains ✕ ○ □ △ · ✓.\n6. **One thing I'd change:** make Mode 7 a first-class depth renderer: shapes with parameters, materials and a hit flash, a lighting pass, and a `pick()` hit test for what it draws.\n\n## Prompt Feedback\n1. **What I liked most:** a clear, testable definition of done, freedom of design, a detailed plan, and one quiet test command.\n2. **What was confusing:** the plan used another run's file names, and the random-title rule conflicted with STAGE.md's \"use the plan's title\". The \"read only what you need\" rule also sat awkwardly with docs that had to be read.\n3. **The hardest thing:** the scope in one sitting, and balancing it so a fair player can finish.\n4. **What I'd change:** split the AI Hub integration from the single-player build, or give a target length for the demo, and let the task file carry the run-specific name tokens."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1791,"cache_read_input_tokens":890743,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1791},"output_tokens":2,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"a0704575-f16b-44ea-a24d-907e9c278025","uuid":"e5b38378-eb1c-4835-b087-d776b4bca21b","timestamp":"2026-11-04T03:49:27.755Z","request_id":"req_011CghCrRz4FEhjQRBtDcj88"}
