{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CghCxbHKRY6cXFSvuZ5tW","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The game is finished, and the stop check's last objection is one I can't resolve by doing more work. It asks for verification that isn't written by me, and no tester, reviewer or tool independent of me is available in this session. Every further check I run would be self-written in exactly the same way. If the evidence below is enough for you, the goal can be cleared; if you want an independent check, tell me who or what should run it.\n\nWhat exists, all shown in this transcript:\n\n- **The task files:** I printed STAGE.md and TASK.md in full and built against them.\n- **The game:** `OperationWolfpack-OperationWolf-claude-opus-5-5-xhigh-20261103-183009.html` is in this folder's root, with `exit-interview.json` beside it. That file parses and has every field filled.\n- **Tests:** `npm test` passes 255 checks with none failing. That includes two complete playthroughs of the whole game, browser checks on the shipped file, and the AI Hub project's own test harness passing in all 5 configurations.\n- **The harness:** that one check is not my code.\n- **Requirement audit:** `npm run audit` passes one check for each TASK.md requirement, 34 in all.\n- **Player-style run:** `tools/verify-browser.mjs` drives the shipped page in a real browser and passed all 8 checks:\n  - an emulated phone showing the controller-required notice, with a working SETTINGS button and the attract demo starting;\n  - a second controller joining mid-operation;\n  - the real Settings screen switching the button prompts to Xbox.\n- **No stubs:** the game code has no TODO or placeholder text, and I removed every empty function.\n- **Dev sliders:** 23 live sliders with a reset to defaults. TASK.md names no count, so that number is my choice.\n\nTwo limits remain: balance was tuned against a bot, not real players, and online play hasn't been tested by people on separate machines.\n\n## Source Information\n- **Date:** 2026-11-03 18:30:09\n- **Source:** Operation Wolf (Taito, 1987), the arcade light-gun shooter with the mounted submachine gun.\n- **Plan:** PLAN.md (written by claude-opus-5-5 at max effort). Where it differed from TASK.md, TASK.md won.\n- **Genre and Perspective:** light-gun rail shooter with bosses, hostage escorts and drop-in co-op. First person on a scripted rail: sideways across the battlefield, forward up a river, and backward off a plane's ramp.\n- **Tech:** my-3D2dge v0.85.2, with 6 edits logged in `engine/CHANGES.md`. AI Hub session client 1.4.0. Plain JavaScript in one HTML file. Node 24 and Playwright 1.63.0 for tests and tools only.\n- **Art Direction:** the engine's native procedural pixel art, plus 66 new shapes, 35 sprites and 23 dressed character rigs. The setting, the Brimstone Coast, is original.\n- **Animation:** the engine's character rig system, plus new poses: hands up, bound hostages, panic, the officer's whistle, a rocket on the shoulder, the collar grab and the villain's cackle. Vehicles, the first-person gun and effects are animated procedurally.\n- **Audio:** all synthesized in code, no audio files: 17 songs and stings, 58 sound effects, and Animalese-style gibberish voices with a wolf howl.\n- **Online:** AI Hub Session Protocol v1, shared-world mode, 1–4 players. Also local drop-in co-op for 1–4 controllers.\n- **Testing:** allowed: headless tools, scripted playthroughs, screenshots for looks only, and no subagents. Used: `npm test` (22 suites, 255 checks), `npm run audit` (34 checks) and `tools/verify-browser.mjs` (8 checks).\n- **Research:** none of my own beyond the given plan (which cites Wikipedia and arcade-history.com) and the engine's and hub's docs.\n- **Environment:** Claude Code CLI (headless); Linux (WSL2); headless Chromium 153 through Playwright.\n- **Model:** Anthropic, claude-opus-5-5, reasoning effort xhigh.\n- **Usage:** cost and token count unknown (not visible to me). Session time is an estimate of about 2 hours 15 minutes, made when I wrote the About pages; the final checks ran later than that.\n\n## Testing Feedback\n1. **How many issues did testing catch?** About 30.\n2. **How many were critical or game-breaking?** About 10:\n   - two bosses never arrived;\n   - the CROCODILE gunboat boss couldn't be killed;\n   - enemy grenades and mortar shells couldn't be shot down;\n   - four crashes;\n   - the playthrough bot lost its input partway through.\n3. **Most helpful test:** the headless whole-game playthrough by a bot with human-like reaction times, on the easiest difficulty with no cheats.\n4. **Least helpful test:** the projection round-trip test. It never failed.\n\n## Engine Feedback\n1. **What did you like most about the engine?** Exact headless determinism, so the whole campaign tests in seconds. Mode 7 with the character rigs gives real depth cheaply, and the standard menus came free.\n2. **Did you get stuck or guess wrong?** Yes:\n   - `m7.shape` ignores shape parameters and materials;\n   - a scene's `view` field clashed with a name I used;\n   - `game.go` fails inside boot steps;\n   - timers are cleared on a scene change;\n   - `E.store` doesn't persist headless;\n   - the font lacked the PlayStation button symbols;\n   - `E.Menu` has no scale option for text read from 10 feet.\n3. **What was the most important thing you had to build yourself?** The light-gun layer on Mode 7: calibrated hit shapes, the deterministic game simulation, six phased bosses, the bot, and the shared-world netcode.\n4. **What was the hardest part about using the engine?** Matching what you hit to what Mode 7 draws, and lighting the night stages under Mode 7.\n5. **What did you change in the engine, and why?** Six edits:\n   - Mode 7 views keyed by whole-degree camera pitch, so a bobbing camera reuses its drawing views;\n   - stereo pan for sound effects;\n   - PlayStation glyphs as the default;\n   - settings rows on any Shell screen;\n   - hidden setting groups;\n   - the font glyphs ✕ ○ □ △ · ✓.\n6. **If you could change one thing about the engine, what would it be?** Make Mode 7 a first-class depth renderer: shapes with parameters, materials and a hit flash, a lighting pass, and a `pick()` hit test.\n\n## Prompt Feedback\n1. **What did you like most about this prompt?** A clear, testable definition of done, freedom of design, a detailed plan, and one quiet test command.\n2. **What was confusing?** The plan used another run's file names. The random-title rule conflicted with STAGE.md's \"use the plan's title\". The \"read only what you need\" rule sat oddly with docs that had to be read.\n3. **What was the hardest thing to do?** The scope in one sitting, and balancing it so a fair player can finish.\n4. **What would you change in this prompt?** Split the AI Hub integration from the single-player build, or give a target demo length. Let the task file carry the run-specific name tokens."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":4,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3601,"cache_read_input_tokens":895717,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":3601},"output_tokens":5,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"a0704575-f16b-44ea-a24d-907e9c278025","uuid":"26b3a67d-f13c-4e46-ba6a-f5f06a2f9e4c","timestamp":"2026-11-04T03:50:53.061Z","request_id":"req_011CghCxaiaoSVd8KTnD8b4m"}
