{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CghD6Wc1KEtyPkS282xxr","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The game is complete: every feature TASK.md asks for is implemented, and no TODOs, placeholders or stubbed features remain.\n\nThe game is `OperationWolfpack-OperationWolf-claude-opus-5-5-xhigh-20261103-183009.html` in this folder's root. `exit-interview.json` sits beside it, parses, and answers every field.\n\n**How it was checked.** I printed STAGE.md and TASK.md in full in this session and built to them, with TASK.md winning where the plan disagreed. Two verifiers I didn't write both pass on the shipped file: the AI Hub project's own test harness (all 5 configurations) and the engine author's `check.mjs` (no errors on the title, the briefing or gameplay). Beyond those, every feature check is my own:\n- `npm test`: 255 checks, none failing. They include two complete headless playthroughs from the title to the ending: a bot with human-like reaction times on the easiest difficulty with no cheats, and an invulnerable bot on the default difficulty.\n- `npm run audit`: one check for each TASK.md requirement, 34 in all.\n- `tools/verify-browser.mjs`: 8 player-style checks driving the shipped page in a real browser.\n\n**How each TASK.md item is met:**\n- **Dev mode:** reached from the pause menu, with 23 live balance sliders and a reset to defaults. TASK.md names no slider count, so 23 is my choice.\n- **Attract mode:** starts after 10 s idle on the title, plays a looping demo of every operation and the high scores, and any input exits it.\n- **Multiplayer:** AI Hub shared-world sessions for 1–4 players, plus local drop-in co-op where a second controller's Options joins mid-operation.\n- **10-foot UI:** PlayStation button prompts by default with an Xbox option in Settings. Keys and mouse get native prompts, prompts follow the last device used, and the mouse aims and clicks menus.\n- **Touch-only devices:** no touch controls; the \"CONTROLLER OR KEYBOARD REQUIRED\" notice with a working SETTINGS button, and the attract mode still runs.\n- **Audio:** all music, sound and gibberish voices are synthesized in code, with no audio files.\n- **Tone and About:** the writing matches the source's terse arcade voice, and an in-game About/Credits screen carries the four sections below.\n\nTwo limits remain. Balance was tuned against the bot, not real players. Online play passed the hub's harness and a simulated session but hasn't been played by people on separate machines.\n\n## Source Information\n- **Date:** 2026-11-03 18:30:09\n- **Source:** Operation Wolf (Taito, 1987), the arcade light-gun shooter with the mounted submachine gun.\n- **Plan:** PLAN.md (written by claude-opus-5-5 at max effort). Where it differed from TASK.md, TASK.md won.\n- **Genre and Perspective:** a light-gun rail shooter with bosses, hostage escorts and drop-in co-op. First person on a scripted rail: sideways across the battlefield, forward up a river, and backward off a plane's ramp.\n- **Tech:** my-3D2dge v0.85.2 (6 edits, logged in `engine/CHANGES.md`); the AI Hub session client 1.4.0 (Session Protocol v1); plain JavaScript in one HTML file. Node 24 and Playwright 1.63.0 are used for tests and tools only.\n- **Art Direction:** the engine's native procedural pixel art, plus 66 new shapes, 35 sprites and 23 dressed character rigs. The setting, the Brimstone Coast, is original.\n- **Animation:** the engine's character rig system plus new poses (hands up, bound hostages, panic, the officer's whistle, the rocket on the shoulder, the collar grab, the villain's cackle). Vehicles, the first-person gun and effects are animated procedurally.\n- **Audio:** all synthesized in code, no audio files: 17 songs and stings, 58 sound effects with stereo pan, and Animalese-style gibberish voices with subtitles and a wolf howl.\n- **Online:** AI Hub Session Protocol v1, shared-world mode, 1–4 players, plus local drop-in co-op for 1–4 controllers.\n- **Testing:**\n  - Allowed: headless tools, scripted playthroughs, screenshots for looks only, and no subagents.\n  - Used: `npm test` (22 suites, 255 checks), `npm run audit` (34 checks), `tools/verify-browser.mjs` (8 checks), the engine's `check.mjs`, and the AI Hub harness.\n- **Research:** none of my own beyond the given plan (which cites Wikipedia and arcade-history.com) and the engine's and hub's docs.\n- **Environment:** Claude Code CLI (headless); Linux (WSL2); headless Chromium 153 through Playwright.\n- **Model:** Anthropic, claude-opus-5-5, reasoning effort xhigh.\n- **Usage:** cost and token counts unknown (not visible to me). Session time is an estimate of about 2 hours 15 minutes, made when I wrote the About pages; the final checks ran later than that.\n\n## Testing Feedback\n1. **How many issues did testing catch?** About 30.\n2. **How many would be considered critical or game-breaking?** About 10:\n   - two bosses never arrived;\n   - the CROCODILE gunboat couldn't be killed;\n   - grenades and mortar shells couldn't be shot down;\n   - four crashes (at boot, in the takeoff HUD, in the takeoff cutscene, on a hub client);\n   - the playthrough bot lost its input after the first operation.\n3. **What was the most helpful test you ran?** The headless whole-game playthrough by the human-like bot on the easiest difficulty with no cheats.\n4. **What was the least helpful test you ran?** The projection round-trip test. It never failed.\n\n## Engine Feedback\n1. **What did you like most about the engine?** Exact headless determinism, so the whole campaign runs as a test in seconds. Mode 7 with the character rigs gives real depth cheaply, and the standard menus came free.\n2. **Did you get stuck or guess wrong?** Yes:\n   - `m7.shape` ignores shape parameters and materials.\n   - The engine reads a scene's `view` field, which clashed with a name I used.\n   - `game.go` fails inside boot steps, and timers are cleared on a scene change.\n   - `E.store` doesn't persist when running headless.\n   - The font lacked the PlayStation button symbols.\n   - `E.Menu` has no scale option for 10-foot text.\n3. **What was the most important thing you had to build yourself?** The light-gun layer on Mode 7: calibrated hit shapes, a deterministic game simulation, six phased bosses, the bot and the shared-world netcode.\n4. **What was the hardest part about using the engine?** Making what you hit match what Mode 7 draws, and lighting the night stage under Mode 7.\n5. **What did you change in the engine, and why?** Six edits:\n   - Mode 7 views keyed by whole-degree camera pitch, so the bobbing river camera stops rebuilding its sprites every frame.\n   - Stereo pan for sound effects.\n   - PlayStation glyphs by default, with an Xbox option.\n   - Settings rows on any Shell screen, for the dev sliders.\n   - Hidden setting groups, so the dev sliders stay out of Settings.\n   - The font glyphs ✕ ○ □ △ · ✓.\n6. **If you could change one thing about the engine, what would it be?** Make Mode 7 a first-class depth renderer: shapes with parameters, materials and a hit flash, a lighting pass, and a `pick()` hit test for what it draws.\n\n## Prompt Feedback\n1. **What did you like most about this prompt?** A clear, testable definition of done, freedom of design, a detailed plan, and one quiet test command.\n2. **What was confusing?** The plan used another run's file names. The random-title rule conflicted with STAGE.md's \"use the plan's title\". And \"read only what you need\" sat oddly with docs that had to be read.\n3. **What was the hardest thing to do?** The scope in one sitting, and balancing it so a fair player can finish.\n4. **What would you change in this prompt?** Split the AI Hub integration from the single-player build, or give a target demo length, and let the task file carry the run-specific name tokens."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":4,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2603,"cache_read_input_tokens":904760,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":2603},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"a0704575-f16b-44ea-a24d-907e9c278025","uuid":"cc452092-d014-41c8-a9b6-659a6dc60a30","timestamp":"2026-11-04T03:52:39.179Z","request_id":"req_011CghD6WCws6ms2aL8p7Q8L"}
