{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CghDJnQrciVFDUjBVDHCF","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Operation Wolfpack is complete. The single file `OperationWolfpack-OperationWolf-claude-opus-5-5-xhigh-20261103-183009.html` is in the root, and `exit-interview.json` parses with every field answered. Every TASK.md feature is implemented, and the game code has no TODOs, placeholders or stubbed features.\n\nTwo checks I didn't write, the AI Hub harness (all 5 configurations) and the engine's `check.mjs`, pass on the shipped file. My own suites also pass: `npm test` (255 checks), `npm run audit` (34) and the browser run (8). Those three cover the rest of the feature list from my reading of TASK.md, so they aren't independent verification.\n\nThe stop check keeps asking for proof of the whole feature list from a verifier other than me. Nothing available in this session can provide that, so further turns will repeat the same evidence.\n\n## Source Information\n- **Date:** 2026-11-03 18:30:09\n- **Source:** Operation Wolf (Taito, 1987)\n- **Plan:** PLAN.md (written by claude-opus-5-5 at max effort); TASK.md won where they differed\n- **Genre and Perspective:** light-gun rail shooter with bosses, hostage escorts and co-op; first person on a scripted rail\n- **Tech:** my-3D2dge v0.85.2 (6 logged edits), AI Hub session client 1.4.0, plain JavaScript in one HTML file; Node 24 and Playwright 1.63.0 for tests only\n- **Art Direction:** the engine's procedural pixel art, plus 66 new shapes, 35 sprites and 23 character rigs; the Brimstone Coast setting is original\n- **Animation:** the engine's character rigs plus new poses; vehicles, the first-person gun and effects are animated procedurally\n- **Audio:** all synthesized in code (17 songs and stings, 58 sound effects, Animalese-style gibberish voices); no audio files\n- **Online:** AI Hub Session Protocol v1, shared-world mode, 1–4 players; local drop-in co-op for 1–4\n- **Testing:** allowed were headless tools, scripted playthroughs, screenshots for looks only, and no subagents; used `npm test`, `npm run audit`, `tools/verify-browser.mjs`, the engine's `check.mjs` and the hub harness\n- **Research:** none beyond the plan and the engine and hub docs\n- **Environment:** Claude Code CLI (headless); Linux (WSL2); headless Chromium 153\n- **Model:** Anthropic, claude-opus-5-5, reasoning effort xhigh\n- **Usage:** cost and tokens unknown; session time about 2 h 15 min, estimated when I wrote the About pages, so the final checks ran longer\n\n## Testing Feedback\n1. **How many issues did testing catch?** About 30.\n2. **How many were critical?** About 10: bosses that never arrived or couldn't be killed, grenades and mortar shells that couldn't be shot down, four crashes, and the test bot losing its input.\n3. **Most helpful test:** the headless whole-game playthrough.\n4. **Least helpful test:** the projection round-trip test, which never failed.\n\n## Engine Feedback\n1. **What I liked most:** exact headless determinism, cheap depth from Mode 7 with the character rigs, and standard menus for free.\n2. **Did I get stuck or guess wrong?** Yes:\n   - `m7.shape` ignores shape parameters and materials.\n   - A scene's `view` field clashed with a name I used.\n   - `game.go` fails during boot, and timers are cleared on a scene change.\n   - Saved data doesn't persist headless.\n   - The font lacked the PlayStation button symbols.\n   - `E.Menu` has no scale option.\n3. **Most important thing I built myself:** the light-gun layer on Mode 7: hit detection, the simulation, the bosses, the bot and the netcode.\n4. **Hardest part:** matching what you hit to what Mode 7 draws, and lighting the night stages.\n5. **What I changed in the engine:** six edits: Mode 7 view caching, stereo pan, PlayStation symbols by default, Shell settings rows, hidden setting groups, and new font glyphs.\n6. **One thing I'd change:** make Mode 7 a first-class depth renderer, with lighting and a `pick()` hit test.\n\n## Prompt Feedback\n1. **What I liked most:** a clear, testable definition of done, freedom of design, a detailed plan, and one quiet test command.\n2. **What was confusing:** the plan used another run's file names; the random-title rule conflicted with STAGE.md; and \"read only what you need\" sat oddly with docs that had to be read.\n3. **Hardest thing:** the scope in one sitting, and balancing it for a fair player.\n4. **What I'd change:** split the AI Hub work from the single-player build, or give a target demo length; and let the task file carry the run-specific name tokens."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":4,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2215,"cache_read_input_tokens":918529,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":2215},"output_tokens":2,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"a0704575-f16b-44ea-a24d-907e9c278025","uuid":"fa0d06a7-1f2f-4748-8447-b7618055f5f9","timestamp":"2026-11-04T03:55:16.845Z","request_id":"req_011CghDJn4X7yewJaLijZqaJ"}
