1, shared-world, 1–4 players; local drop-in co-op for 1–4\n- **Testing:**\n  - Allowed: headless tools, scripted playthroughs, screenshots for looks only, and no subagents.\n  - Used: `npm test`, `npm run audit`, `tools/verify-browser.mjs`, the engine's `check.mjs` and the hub harness.\n- **Research:** none beyond the plan and the engine and hub docs\n- **Environment:** Claude Code CLI (headless); Linux (WSL2); headless Chromium 153\n- **Model:** Anthropic, claude-opus-5-5, reasoning effort xhigh\n- **Usage:** cost and tokens unknown; session time about 2 h 15 min, estimated when I wrote the About pages, so it doesn't include the final checks\n\n## Testing Feedback\n1. **How many issues did testing catch?** About 30.\n2. **How many were critical?** About 10: bosses that never arrived or couldn't be killed, projectiles that couldn't be shot down, four crashes, and the test bot losing its input.\n3. **Most helpful test:** the headless whole-game playthrough.\n4. **Least helpful test:** the projection round-trip test, which never failed.\n\n## Engine Feedback\n1. **What I liked most:** exact headless determinism, cheap depth from Mode 7 with the character rigs, and standard menus for free.\n2. **Did I get stuck or guess wrong?** Yes:\n   - `m7.shape` ignores shape parameters and materials.\n   - A scene's `view` field clashed with my code.\n   - `game.go` fails during boot, and timers are cleared on a scene change.\n   - Saved data doesn't persist headless.\n   - The font lacks PlayStation button symbols.\n   - `E.Menu` has no scale option.\n3. **Most important thing I built myself:** the light-gun layer on Mode 7: hit detection, the simulation, the bosses, the bot and the netcode.\n4. **Hardest part:** making what you hit match what Mode 7 draws, and the night lighting.\n5. **What I changed in the engine:** six edits: Mode 7 view caching, stereo pan, PlayStation symbols by default, Shell settings rows, hidden setting groups, and new font glyphs.\n6. **One thing I'd change:** make Mode 7 a first-class depth renderer, with lighting and a `pick()` hit test.\n\n## Prompt Feedback\n1. **What I liked most:** a clear, testable definition of done, freedom of design, a detailed plan, and one quiet test command.\n2. **What was confusing:** the plan used another run's file names; the random-title rule conflicted with STAGE.md's \"use the title it gives\"; \"read only what you need\" sat oddly with docs that had to be read.\n3. **Hardest thing:** the scope in one sitting, and balancing it for a fair player.\n4. **What I'd change:** split the AI Hub work from the single-player build, or give a target demo length; let the task file carry the run-specific name tokens."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":4,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2251,"cache_read_input_tokens":932244,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":2251},"output_tokens":6,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"a0704575-f16b-44ea-a24d-907e9c278025","uuid":"ec027770-c71c-4a31-b42c-71936ed8c48d","timestamp":"2026-11-04T03:57:31.777Z","request_id":"req_011CghDUman4fQFigKLgmzq5"}
