{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CghDb6vXrTzrMrTwcGxSo","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Operation Wolfpack is finished; nothing has changed since my last reply.\n\n- **The game:** `OperationWolfpack-OperationWolf-claude-opus-5-5-xhigh-20261103-183009.html` is in the folder's root.\n- **Exit interview:** `exit-interview.json` parses and answers every field.\n- **Features:** every TASK.md feature is implemented, with no TODOs, placeholders or stubbed features in the game code.\n- **Checks I didn't write:** the AI Hub harness (all 5 configurations) and the engine's `check.mjs` (no errors) both pass, but they only cover part of the feature list.\n- **My own suites:** these cover the rest, and all pass:\n  - `npm test`: 255 checks.\n  - `npm run audit`: 34 checks, one per TASK.md requirement.\n  - `tools/verify-browser.mjs`: 8 checks.\n- **Not yet tested:** balance with real players (it was tuned against a bot), and online play by people on separate machines.\n\nNothing available here can independently verify the features my own suites cover, so these repeated checks can't pass. `/goal clear` ends the loop.\n\n## Source Information\n- **Date:** 2026-11-03 18:30:09\n- **Source:** Operation Wolf (Taito, 1987)\n- **Plan:** PLAN.md (claude-opus-5-5, max effort); TASK.md won where they differed\n- **Genre and Perspective:** light-gun rail shooter with bosses, hostage escorts and co-op; first person on a scripted rail\n- **Tech:** my-3D2dge v0.85.2 (6 logged edits), AI Hub session client 1.4.0, plain JavaScript in one HTML file; Node 24 and Playwright 1.63.0 for tests only\n- **Art Direction:** the engine's procedural pixel art, plus 66 new shapes, 35 sprites and 23 character rigs; the setting, the Brimstone Coast, is original\n- **Animation:** the engine's character rigs plus new poses; vehicles, the first-person gun and effects are animated procedurally\n- **Audio:** all synthesized in code: 17 songs and stings, 58 sound effects, Animalese-style gibberish voices; no audio files\n- **Online:** AI Hub Session Protocol v1, shared-world mode, 1–4 players; local drop-in co-op for 1–4\n- **Testing:** allowed were headless tools, scripted playthroughs, screenshots for looks only, and no subagents; I used `npm test`, `npm run audit`, `tools/verify-browser.mjs`, the engine's `check.mjs` and the hub harness\n- **Research:** none beyond the plan and the engine and hub docs\n- **Environment:** Claude Code CLI (headless); Linux (WSL2); headless Chromium 153\n- **Model:** Anthropic, claude-opus-5-5, reasoning effort xhigh\n- **Usage:** cost and tokens unknown; session time about 2 h 15 min, estimated while writing the About pages (the final checks ran later)\n\n## Testing Feedback\n1. **How many issues did testing catch?** About 30.\n2. **How many were critical?** About 10: bosses that never arrived or couldn't be killed, projectiles that couldn't be shot down, four crashes, and the test bot losing its input.\n3. **Most helpful test:** the headless whole-game playthrough.\n4. **Least helpful test:** the projection round-trip test, which never failed.\n\n## Engine Feedback\n1. **What I liked most:** exact headless determinism, cheap depth from Mode 7 with the character rigs, and standard menus for free.\n2. **Did I get stuck or guess wrong?** Yes:\n   - `m7.shape` ignores shape parameters and materials.\n   - A scene's `view` field clashed with my code.\n   - `game.go` fails during boot, and timers are cleared when the scene changes.\n   - Saved data doesn't persist when running headless.\n   - The font lacks the PlayStation button symbols.\n   - `E.Menu` has no scale option.\n3. **Most important thing I built myself:** the light-gun layer on Mode 7: hit detection, the simulation, the bosses, the bot and the netcode.\n4. **Hardest part:** making what you hit match what Mode 7 draws, and night lighting.\n5. **What I changed in the engine:** six edits: Mode 7 view caching, stereo pan, PlayStation symbols by default, Shell settings rows, hidden setting groups, and new font glyphs.\n6. **One thing I'd change:** make Mode 7 a first-class depth renderer, with lighting and a `pick()` hit test.\n\n## Prompt Feedback\n1. **What I liked most:** a clear, testable definition of done, freedom of design, a detailed plan, and one quiet test command.\n2. **What was confusing:** the plan used another run's file names; the random-title rule conflicted with STAGE.md; and \"read only what you need\" sat awkwardly with docs that had to be read.\n3. **Hardest thing:** the scope in one sitting, and balancing it for a fair player.\n4. **What I'd change:** split the AI Hub work from the single-player build, or give a target demo length; let the task file carry the run-specific name tokens."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":4,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2163,"cache_read_input_tokens":941110,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":2163},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"a0704575-f16b-44ea-a24d-907e9c278025","uuid":"429e88fb-1fbd-4944-8ec4-7b819b2150c7","timestamp":"2026-11-04T03:58:57.809Z","request_id":"req_011CghDb6Zx6XUojNRuTtBJ1"}
